window.SB_STUDY = {
  quiz: [
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Roadmap", n: "partA", q: "Which part of the roadmap is the vocabulary you need before anything else, and what does it tell you to do with chapter 3?", opts: ["Foundations (ch 1-4) - read chapter 3 twice, because DI is the concept the whole book leans on", "Boot Basics (ch 5-9) - start.spring.io and your first runnable app", "Advanced (ch 22-28) - AOP, security, auto-configuration and caching", "Practice (ch 29-34) - unit tests, slices, integration tests and Docker"], a: 0, e: "Dependency injection is the load-bearing idea, so the roadmap explicitly says to read it twice. Boot Basics is about getting a first app running, Advanced is about being a good citizen in someone else's application, and Practice is about shipping - all of them come after the vocabulary." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Roadmap", n: "partC", q: "Which roadmap part owns @RestController, @Valid and status codes, and what has to come first in that part?", opts: ["Web and REST (ch 10-15); Boot Basics comes first because HTTP only means something once an app actually runs", "Foundations (ch 1-4); Web and REST comes first because annotations are hard without a JVM", "Data (ch 16-21); Web and REST comes first because the browser is the only data client", "Advanced (ch 22-28); Web and REST comes first because security sits in front of every endpoint"], a: 0, e: "The web part is where DispatcherServlet and the filter chain finally make sense, and that requires something to dispatch to. The other parts reorder it: the browser is not the only client, security is only one chapter of Advanced, and annotations do not need a running app to be understandable." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Roadmap", n: "partD", q: "Which part contains the N+1 trap and the transaction model, and where does the roadmap say the real performance wins live?", opts: ["Data (ch 16-21); the real wins are in the persistence layer, not in the web layer", "Advanced (ch 22-28); performance work is really a profiler exercise", "Web and REST (ch 10-15); serializing DTOs is where latency is won", "Practice (ch 29-34); only integration tests reveal real performance"], a: 0, e: "The Data part exists to prevent the N+1 trap and to get the transaction model right, and it says the real performance wins are there. Turning on a profiler is part of Advanced, DTO shape is only a small slice of latency, and tests are not where you find performance." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Roadmap", n: "partF", q: "Which part is the one where the roadmap tells you to stop reading docs and start reading source?", opts: ["Practice (ch 29-34): unit tests, slices, integration tests, Docker packaging and deployment", "Advanced (ch 22-28): caching and auto-configuration internals", "Data (ch 16-21): reading Hibernate's source for fetch strategies", "Foundations (ch 1-4): reading the IoC container source"], a: 0, e: "The Practice part is explicitly the point where you read source instead of docs, because that is where you own the whole pipeline: tests, packaging and deployment. Advanced and Data still have documentation that answers most questions, and Foundations is learnable from any Spring book." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Startup", n: "main", q: "You write main() calling SpringApplication.run(App.class, args). Which statement about that call is correct?", opts: ["It returns a fully refreshed ApplicationContext whose web server is already bound and listening", "It returns a plain context object with no beans registered yet", "main() returns null and the container starts lazily on the first HTTP request", "refresh() only registers bean definitions; singleton instantiation happens later"], a: 0, e: "By the time run() returns, the environment is prepared, the context is refreshed - which is the step that instantiates and wires singletons - and the embedded server is accepting traffic, which is why the last two options are wrong. main() also has to return void, so there is nothing for it to return." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Startup", n: "run", q: "Put the SpringApplication.run() phases in the correct order.", opts: ["banner and environment -> create context and load bean definitions -> run auto-configuration -> instantiate and wire beans (refresh) -> start web server -> ApplicationReadyEvent", "create context -> prepare environment -> auto-configure -> banner -> refresh", "refresh -> auto-configure -> environment -> banner -> server", "environment -> auto-configure -> refresh -> banner -> server"], a: 0, e: "Environment and banner come first because listeners and profile-specific config read the environment; refresh instantiates the singletons, which is where @Bean methods and auto-configurations meet; the server is up before ApplicationReadyEvent, which is the documented 'ready for requests and runners' signal. Every other ordering breaks a dependency - you cannot condition on properties before the environment exists, and you cannot log a banner from a bean that was never created." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Startup", n: "autoconfig", q: "Where does Spring Boot 3 read its list of auto-configuration candidates from, and what is the decisive criterion for inclusion?", opts: ["META-INF/spring/org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration.imports; only the classes named by the jars actually on your classpath are candidates", "spring.factories, which Boot 3 still reads before the imports file", "A classpath scan for every @Configuration class in every jar", "The imports file plus spring.factories, merged in alphabetical order"], a: 0, e: "The imports file is the Boot 2.7+/3.x mechanism, and auto-configuration registration from spring.factories was removed in 3.0, so options 2 and 4 describe a world that no longer exists. A component scan does not pick up auto-configurations from other jars - it only finds @Configuration classes under your base package - so a missing bean is usually a missing dependency or a missing property, not a missing scan." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Startup", n: "conditional", q: "Startup fails with 'Failed to configure a DataSource: 'url' attribute is not specified and no embedded datasource could be configured.' Which checks, in order, are most likely to find the cause?", opts: ["Is a JDBC starter on the classpath, and is spring.datasource.url set to a reachable value", "Is @SpringBootApplication in a package above your repositories", "Is port 8080 free", "Do your entities have @Entity"], a: 0, e: "DataSourceAutoConfiguration is @ConditionalOnClass(DataSource) and @ConditionalOnMissingBean, and DataSourceProperties binds spring.datasource.url - so a missing starter plus a missing url produces exactly this message. Package placement causes a component-scan miss reported as a missing bean, the port problem surfaces later as 'Port already in use', and @Entity only matters once a DataSource exists." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Startup", n: "conditional", q: "An auto-configuration is annotated @Bean @ConditionalOnMissingBean(DataSource.class). What does that condition actually promise?", opts: ["The auto-config backs off and declares no bean if the application has already defined a DataSource - it is a back-off, not a guarantee", "The bean is always created and any application bean of that type is replaced by it", "The bean is created only if the application defines a DataSource", "The condition is evaluated on every method call at runtime"], a: 0, e: "@ConditionalOnMissingBean is a back-off: your bean wins and the auto-config quietly steps aside, which is exactly what lets you override auto-configuration without excluding anything. It is a bean-definition-time condition, not a per-call runtime check, and it is order-sensitive - conditions are evaluated during configuration class parsing, so auto-configurations that depend on each other use @AutoConfigureBefore/After rather than relying on scan order." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Startup", n: "conflict", q: "Startup dies with NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException: 'expected single matching bean but found 2: emailSenderSmtp, emailSenderSes'. Which fix is the least surprising?", opts: ["Mark the preferred implementation @Primary, or name it at the injection point with @Qualifier(\"emailSenderSmtp\")", "Add @Autowired(required = false) to both fields", "Delete one of the two @Bean methods", "Set spring.main.allow-bean-definition-overriding=true"], a: 0, e: "@Primary and @Qualifier both express intent: here is the one I mean. required = false turns a startup-time error into a null at runtime, which is worse; deleting a bean method deletes a feature; and allow-bean-definition-overriding controls definition overriding, not ambiguity - it will not fix this and it silently breaks ordering-sensitive auto-configurations." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Startup", n: "nobean", q: "Constructor injection fails with 'Parameter 0 of constructor in OrderService required a bean of type PaymentClient that could not be found'. Order the checks from most to least likely.", opts: ["(1) is PaymentClient implemented by a class annotated @Component/@Service, (2) is that class inside or below your @SpringBootApplication package, (3) is the @Bean method that creates it conditional or in another profile, (4) is the client library itself on the classpath", "Check the heap size, the free port and the database first, because a missing bean message is usually a resource problem", "Re-run with --debug and read the Condition Evaluation Report, which will name the failing condition", "Delete the constructor and use field injection so the context starts even when the dependency is absent"], a: 0, e: "Missing-bean errors are structural, not environmental: a missing stereotype, a package outside the component-scan root, a bean disabled by a condition or profile, and only then a missing dependency. Heap, port, database and cache problems produce completely different messages. The condition evaluation report from --debug is a great tool, but it reports on conditions - a bean that is simply not registered as a component will not appear there as a failed condition." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Startup", n: "wire", q: "Which injection style lets the container use constructor arguments to satisfy dependencies and fail fast at startup if a dependency is missing?", opts: ["Constructor injection - dependencies become final fields and the context refuses to start when one is unsatisfied", "Field injection with @Autowired - missing fields stay null until first use", "Setter injection - the object is created first and wired afterwards, so gaps are invisible", "Field injection plus a null check in every method"], a: 0, e: "Constructor injection makes the dependency a required part of the object's identity: final fields, no partially constructed object, and a fail-fast context. Field and setter injection defer the problem to runtime, where you get a NullPointerException in production rather than a clear startup error." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Startup", n: "portbusy", q: "The log ends with 'Web server failed to start. Port 8080 was already in use.' What actually happened, and what are your real options?", opts: ["Another process holds the socket; change server.port, or find and stop the holder, or use server.port=0 for a random free port in tests", "Spring Boot never starts on a busy port - it retries forever until it succeeds", "Tomcat bound to IPv6 only, so the port is 'reserved'", "The context failed to refresh, which is why the server could not bind"], a: 0, e: "The connector fails fast and kills the context - there is no retry loop and it is not an IPv6 quirk. In tests, port 0 is the idiomatic fix because it lets the OS pick a free port and lets you inject server.port into the test. In production you want either a fixed port from configuration or a service-level port assignment, not a random one." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Startup", n: "ready", q: "The log shows 'Tomcat started on port(s): 8080 (http)' followed by 'Started App in 4.231 seconds'. What do those two lines prove - and what do they not prove?", opts: ["The context refreshed and the connector is bound, so traffic is accepted; they say nothing about Actuator readiness or health indicators, which can still be DOWN", "The JVM started; the port may still be unbound", "The database connection pool is warm and all migrations ran", "Scheduled tasks and ApplicationRunner runners have completed"], a: 0, e: "Those two lines are about the servlet container and the refresh, nothing more. Database connectivity shows up in health indicators and in the Hikari pool log, migration status comes from Flyway or Liquibase, and runners run before the ready event but their failures would have killed startup. Under Kubernetes the thing that decides traffic is the readiness probe, which can be DOWN while these lines look perfectly healthy." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Bean_Lifecycle", n: "populate", q: "A bean's @PostConstruct method calls a @Autowired repository. Why is that guaranteed to work?", opts: ["Because populateBean (dependency injection) completes before any initialization callback, so the injected field is already set", "Because @PostConstruct runs before the constructor", "Because the repository is created lazily on first method call", "Because @PostConstruct is invoked by the container after the whole context is refreshed"], a: 0, e: "The order is instantiate (constructor) -> populate (injection) -> initialize (@PostConstruct, afterPropertiesSet, init method), so injected fields are ready. @PostConstruct is called by a BeanPostProcessor during initialization of that specific bean, not after the whole context refreshes, which is why the other two orderings are wrong - and why calling a bean from another bean's constructor is still unsafe." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Bean_Lifecycle", n: "postconstruct", q: "Which callback runs FIRST among @PostConstruct, InitializingBean.afterPropertiesSet() and a custom init method declared with @Bean(initMethod = \"init\")?", opts: ["@PostConstruct", "InitializingBean.afterPropertiesSet()", "The custom init method", "They all run at the same point in a defined order only if the bean implements Ordered"], a: 0, e: "CommonAnnotationBeanPostProcessor invokes @PostConstruct in its postProcessBeforeInitialization; the container then calls afterPropertiesSet on InitializingBean, and only then the custom init method. That ordering matters in practice: an @PostConstruct method that assumes the custom init method already ran is a bug waiting to happen." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Bean_Lifecycle", n: "afterinit", q: "What is the documented execution order of the initialization callbacks on a bean?", opts: ["@PostConstruct -> BeanNameAware and other Aware callbacks' post-processing -> InitializingBean.afterPropertiesSet -> custom init-method", "BeanNameAware -> @PostConstruct -> custom init-method -> afterPropertiesSet", "custom init-method -> afterPropertiesSet -> @PostConstruct", "All callbacks run in parallel if the bean is lazy"], a: 0, e: "Aware callbacks (BeanNameAware, BeanClassLoaderAware, ApplicationContextAware and friends) are invoked by the container before the initialization callbacks, @PostConstruct is first among those, InitializingBean comes next, and the custom init method is last. Laziness changes when the lifecycle runs, not its order." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Bean_Lifecycle", n: "aware", q: "A bean wants a reference to the ApplicationContext so it can look up beans dynamically. Which callback gives it that, and what is the main smell?", opts: ["ApplicationContextAware; holding the context in a field is a service locator that hides dependencies and is hard to test", "BeanNameAware; it exposes the context, not the name", "@PostConstruct; the context is passed as a parameter there", "DisposableBean; it is a shutdown callback, not an injection point"], a: 0, e: "ApplicationContextAware is the documented hook for a context reference, and using it for dynamic lookup is the classic service-locator anti-pattern: the class now secretly depends on the whole container. Inject the specific collaborator instead, and if you truly need programmatic lookup, inject ApplicationContext deliberately and narrowly rather than hiding it behind business methods." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Bean_Lifecycle", n: "destroyed", q: "On shutdown, which order do the destruction callbacks fire in?", opts: ["@PreDestroy -> DisposableBean.destroy() -> custom destroy-method", "custom destroy-method -> DisposableBean.destroy() -> @PreDestroy", "DisposableBean.destroy() -> @PreDestroy -> custom destroy-method", "Only DisposableBean is called if the bean implements it"], a: 0, e: "The container calls @PreDestroy first, then DisposableBean, then the custom destroy method, in that order, on the way down. If a bean implements DisposableBean, that callback still runs alongside the annotation - the options that make them mutually exclusive are wrong - and beans are destroyed in reverse dependency order, so your dependencies outlive the beans that use them." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Bean_Lifecycle", n: "ready", q: "Which event tells you the context is fully refreshed, the server is up, and CommandLineRunner/ApplicationRunner beans have finished?", opts: ["ApplicationReadyEvent", "ApplicationStartingEvent", "ContextRefreshedEvent", "ApplicationFailedEvent"], a: 0, e: "ApplicationReadyEvent is the last lifecycle event of a successful startup - context refreshed, runners executed, server listening. ApplicationStartingEvent is the very first, before anything is prepared; ContextRefreshedEvent fires when the context finishes refreshing but before runners run and before the server is fully announced; ApplicationFailedEvent means startup broke." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Layers", n: "dto", q: "Should a @RestController return a JPA entity directly? Give the best reason.", opts: ["No - map to a DTO, because entities leak lazy-loading and persistence concerns into your API contract and force a DB change to become an API change", "No - return a Map so the shape can change freely", "Yes - entities are already plain POJOs with getters and setters", "Yes, as long as the entity is annotated @JsonIgnoreProperties(ignoreUnknown = true)"], a: 0, e: "Entities are a persistence model with lazy proxies, bidirectional back-references and a schema that has no business changing your public API. A DTO is the boundary that lets the schema evolve independently, keeps validation and OpenAPI annotations where they belong, and avoids serializing anything you did not intend. Returning a Map just moves the problem to untyped JSON with no compiler help." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Layers", n: "tx", q: "Which layer should own the @Transactional annotation, and why there?", opts: ["The service layer, because the transaction boundary is a business use-case decision and only the service knows the unit of work", "The repository, because the database is what actually commits", "The controller, because it is the only place that knows when the request ends", "Whichever layer is called first, since the boundary is where the proxy sits"], a: 0, e: "The service method is the use case, so it is the natural unit of work; the repository is a mechanism inside it, and the controller is an adapter that must not own atomicity across multiple service calls. Note the fourth option is a trap: the boundary is the annotation on a proxied public method, not the first method that happens to run." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Layers", n: "controller", q: "What does @RestController change compared to @Controller?", opts: ["Every handler return value is written to the response body as JSON instead of being resolved as a view name", "It enables bean validation on the parameters", "It makes the class a singleton instead of a request-scoped bean", "It bypasses the DispatcherServlet and handles the request directly"], a: 0, e: "@RestController is @Controller plus @ResponseBody at class level, so return values are serialized (usually by Jackson) rather than treated as view names. Validation comes from @Valid or @Validated, request scope comes from @Scope, and the request still goes through the DispatcherServlet - the other three options describe unrelated features." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Layers", n: "repository", q: "You declare interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, Long> with no implementation class. How do calls on it work?", opts: ["Spring Data generates a runtime proxy that implements your interface and derives queries from method names or @Query", "The compiler synthesizes an implementation class at build time", "JpaRepository has a static factory you must call manually", "Spring injects a raw EntityManager, so your method names are irrelevant"], a: 0, e: "Spring Data repositories are runtime proxies (JDK dynamic proxies, because the interface is proxied) with a query-dispatch strategy per method family - findBy, deleteBy, @Query, @Modifying. Nothing is generated at compile time, no manual factory is involved, and a raw EntityManager is what the proxy uses under the hood rather than what you use." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Layers", n: "aop", q: "In a layered design, where does the AOP proxy for a service bean sit?", opts: ["Outside the service bean, between the caller and the target - so the caller holds a proxy reference, never the raw target", "Inside the service, wrapping its repository calls", "In the controller, wrapping the service field", "In the DispatcherServlet, wrapping every handler invocation"], a: 0, e: "Spring AOP is proxy-based and outside-in: the container registers the proxy, and injecting into clients gives them the proxy. That single fact explains self-invocation problems, why @Transactional must sit on a public method, and why advice is not applied to internal private calls. Filters and interceptors are the place for cross-cutting web concerns; the AOP proxy is for beans." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Request_Path", n: "filter", q: "Where does a servlet Filter run relative to the DispatcherServlet, and can a Filter rewrite a request before Spring MVC sees it?", opts: ["Before the DispatcherServlet, in the servlet container's chain - it can wrap the request and change the URI before any handler mapping runs", "After the DispatcherServlet, just before the response is written", "Inside handler execution, between the argument resolvers and the controller method", "Only after the response has been committed"], a: 0, e: "Filters are the outermost layer: the container builds the filter chain, the DispatcherServlet sits at the end of it, and only then does Spring MVC map and invoke a handler. That ordering is why filters can read or wrap the HttpServletRequest and add MDC or security context, and why a HandlerInterceptor - which runs inside the DispatcherServlet - is too late to rewrite the request path." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Request_Path", n: "dispatcher", q: "Which component turns an HttpServletRequest into a call to your annotated controller method?", opts: ["DispatcherServlet, via HandlerMapping, HandlerAdapter and argument resolvers", "The embedded Tomcat connector", "A servlet Filter, by reflection", "The @RestController itself, via a proxy"], a: 0, e: "DispatcherServlet is the front controller: HandlerMapping finds the handler, HandlerAdapter invokes it, HandlerMethodArgumentResolver supplies the parameters, and return value handlers serialize the result. The connector only reads bytes off the socket, a Filter runs earlier in the chain, and the controller class is just the handler that gets found." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Request_Path", n: "service", q: "A GET /orders request arrives. Put the hops in order, naming what happens at the transaction boundary.", opts: ["browser -> servlet filters -> DispatcherServlet -> controller -> service (@Transactional proxy opens tx) -> repository -> database; the transaction commits when the transactional service method returns", "browser -> controller -> service -> database -> filters -> DispatcherServlet", "browser -> DispatcherServlet -> filters -> controller -> service -> database (tx commits at the database write)", "browser -> repository -> database -> service -> controller -> DispatcherServlet (reverse order)"], a: 0, e: "Filters wrap the DispatcherServlet, which finds the controller; the transactional service method opens the transaction, the repository uses it, and the commit happens as the proxied method returns - before the controller writes the response. A commit at the database write would break atomicity, and request handling never flows bottom-up." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Request_Path", n: "db", q: "Why does the transaction commit before the HTTP response is written, and what breaks if you rely on writing the response inside the transaction?", opts: ["The transaction ends when the transactional method returns, so a slow or failing response write happens after commit - a broken response does not roll back the data", "The commit is triggered by the connection returning to the pool, which happens after the response", "The response is always flushed before the service method returns", "The transaction ends when the database detects a closed socket"], a: 0, e: "The @Transactional proxy commits on the way out of the method, and only then does the controller serialize and write the response. Holding the transaction open across serialization wastes a pooled connection and still cannot give you a two-phase commit between the database and the socket. The options claiming the pool or the socket drives commit are not how transaction managers work." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_AOP_Proxy", n: "proxy", q: "In Spring Boot, what decides JDK dynamic proxy versus CGLIB for a proxied bean, and what breaks a CGLIB proxy?", opts: ["Boot defaults proxyTargetClass=true, so CGLIB subclasses the class; final classes, final methods, and private methods cannot be advised", "The JDK proxy is used whenever the class implements any interface; CGLIB cannot be used with proxies", "Both are chosen at random and it does not matter", "CGLIB is used only for classes with no methods"], a: 0, e: "With proxyTargetClass=true the container subclasses the target, which requires a non-final class and non-final methods that advice can override. Interface-based JDK proxies are used when you turn that off (and are what Spring Data repositories use), and they require the call to go through the interface. Private methods are invisible to both - a CGLIB proxy is a subclass, not an instrumented class." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_AOP_Proxy", n: "caller", q: "What must be true about the caller for @Transactional advice on a service method to execute at all?", opts: ["The caller must invoke the method through the proxy - that is, an injected bean reference or self-injection, not a direct new or a this call", "The method must be public and the class must be public", "The caller must be in a different package", "The class must implement an interface"], a: 0, e: "The advice lives in the proxy; a direct new Service() or a this.otherMethod() call never touches the proxy, so no advice runs. Public plus non-final is necessary for the proxy to be able to override the method, and the interface is only relevant if you use JDK proxies - but none of those make the call proxied." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_AntiPatterns", n: "selfinv", q: "methodA() calls this.methodB(), and only methodB() is annotated @Transactional. What happens at runtime?", opts: ["No transaction is started: the self-invocation bypasses the proxy, and the database calls run in auto-commit mode", "A transaction starts at methodA because the proxy is already in the call stack", "A transaction starts at methodB because annotations are evaluated on the target class", "A TransactionRequiredException is thrown"], a: 0, e: "This is the self-invocation anti-pattern: the proxy only intercepts calls that come from outside, so the work still happens but with no surrounding transaction, and it auto-commits statement by statement. Fix it by moving the annotated method to another bean, self-injecting the proxy, or restructuring so the annotated method is the one invoked externally. The failure is silent, which is what makes it expensive." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_AOP_Proxy", n: "tx", q: "You add @Transactional to a private method. What is the effect?", opts: ["None - a private method cannot be overridden by a proxy, so no transaction is started", "A transaction starts, because annotations are read from the target class", "A transaction starts only when the class implements an interface", "The application fails to start because the annotation is illegal on a private method"], a: 0, e: "Spring's AOP advice is applied through method interception, and neither a CGLIB subclass nor a JDK proxy can intercept a private call that never leaves the object. Startup is fine and the annotation is legal; it is simply inert. The visible symptom is data being written with no transaction - partial commits and no rollback." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_AOP_Proxy", n: "service", q: "A service class is declared final and every method is final. @Transactional is present and the app starts. What is the likely runtime symptom?", opts: ["No transactions at all: CGLIB cannot subclass a final class, so the proxy falls back to nothing and writes auto-commit", "The application fails to start with a clear error about final classes", "Transactions work, because Hibernate manages its own connections", "Transactions work for reads but silently fail for writes"], a: 0, e: "Making the class final quietly disables the proxy rather than failing loudly, so every method runs with no transaction - the most expensive kind of bug, because it only shows up under concurrent failure. A clear startup error is what you would get from a bad property or a duplicate bean, not from final. Caching and @Async have the same failure mode." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_AOP_Proxy", n: "repo", q: "Does @Transactional on a Spring Data repository method work, and how does it interact with a transactional service method?", opts: ["Yes - the repository is proxied too, and a default REQUIRED call just joins the service's existing transaction", "No - only service beans can be transactional", "Yes, but the repository always opens a second, independent transaction", "Yes, but only for read methods, never for save or delete"], a: 0, e: "Spring Data repositories are proxies, and the SimpleJpaRepository base implementation is transactional, so annotating a custom repository method works and REQUIRED joins whatever transaction is already active. Opening a second transaction would be REQUIRES_NEW, and reads are transactional too - the absence of transactions on reads is exactly what causes lazy-loading failures outside a request." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_AutoConfiguration", n: "classpath", q: "What is the first input that decides which auto-configurations are even candidates?", opts: ["Your classpath - only the auto-configurations named in the imports files of the jars you actually depend on are considered", "Your application.properties file", "The @SpringBootApplication scan of your own packages", "The order of your @Component classes"], a: 0, e: "Auto-configuration is classpath-driven: start.spring.io-generated starters pull in auto-configurations, and those back off again when their @ConditionalOnClass or @ConditionalOnProperty checks fail. Properties only influence conditions after a class is a candidate, and your own packages are scanned by the component scan, which is a different mechanism entirely." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_AutoConfiguration", n: "imports", q: "Boot 3 loads auto-configuration from a file under META-INF/spring. Why does this matter when you move a project from Boot 2.7?", opts: ["Auto-configuration entries moved from spring.factories to the .imports file, so a hand-rolled spring.factories list is silently ignored in Boot 3", "The .imports file is only used for test slices", "The file must be named spring-autoconfigure-metadata.properties", "Nothing changed; the file is just a convention for tooling"], a: 0, e: "If you wrote your own auto-configuration for Boot 2.7 and registered it in spring.factories, Boot 3 will not see it - the entry has to be in META-INF/spring/org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration.imports. Other mechanism files still exist: spring.factories still carries EnvironmentPostProcessor and ApplicationListener registrations, and the metadata file is for tooling, not loading." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_AutoConfiguration", n: "ordering", q: "Two auto-configurations both define a bean that another one @ConditionalOnBean-depends on. Which annotations control the evaluation order?", opts: ["@AutoConfigureBefore / @AutoConfigureAfter (or @AutoConfigureOrder to sort a list) - conditions are evaluated while configuration classes are parsed", "@Order on the @Bean methods", "The alphabetical order of the class names", "@DependsOn on the @Bean methods"], a: 0, e: "Conditions are evaluated during configuration class parsing, so ordering is expressed with the @AutoConfigureBefore/@AutoConfigureAfter family; @AutoConfigureOrder sorts candidates that implement the order interface. @Order is for bean method/instance ordering within a context and does not sequence condition evaluation, and alphabetical order is not a guarantee even though it often looks like one." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_AutoConfiguration", n: "beancond", q: "What is the difference between @ConditionalOnMissingBean and @ConditionalOnBean?", opts: ["OnMissingBean backs off when the application already defines the bean; OnBean requires the bean to already be defined, so it is order-sensitive and risky on user beans", "They are aliases with different default attribute values", "OnMissingBean only works on @Configuration classes, OnBean only on @Bean methods", "OnBean is the Boot 3 replacement for OnMissingBean"], a: 0, e: "The pair is deliberate: an auto-configuration uses OnMissingBean so yours wins, and uses OnBean only for its own internal beans, never for beans defined by the application, because a user bean may be registered after your condition is evaluated. Putting OnBean on a user bean is the classic ordering bug - it works in one test and fails in another depending on import order." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_AutoConfiguration", n: "backoff", q: "You want your own DataSource to win over Boot's. What is the idiomatic Boot 3 way?", opts: ["Define a DataSource bean yourself - DataSourceAutoConfiguration's @ConditionalOnMissingBean makes it back off - and bind spring.datasource.* into it with @ConfigurationProperties", "Set spring.autoconfigure.exclude=DataSourceAutoConfiguration and define the bean yourself", "Override the static createDataSource method by declaring a bean with the same name in a different package", "Add @Primary to your repository"], a: 0, e: "The back-off condition is the designed extension point: your bean is defined, the auto-config steps aside, and you still get relaxed binding and validation by binding the same properties. Excluding works but throws away the auto-configuration's helpful failure messages and defaulting, same-name overriding is an override conflict, and @Primary on a repository has nothing to do with DataSource selection." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_AutoConfiguration", n: "propcond", q: "What does @ConditionalOnProperty(name = \"app.messaging.enabled\", havingValue = \"true\") do when the property is missing entirely?", opts: ["The bean is not registered, because a missing property is treated as not matching havingValue", "The bean is registered with the property defaulting to true", "Startup fails with a placeholder resolution error", "The bean is registered but its @Value fields stay null"], a: 0, e: "The condition compares the resolved value; absent means no match, so matchIfMissing=false (the default) skips the bean. That is why production configs so often fail to start after a rename, and why matchIfMissing exists for deliberate defaults. Missing properties never throw at condition time - placeholders only fail later, when the bean is being created." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_AutoConfiguration", n: "webcond", q: "One auto-configuration must apply to servlet stacks only, and another to reactive stacks only. How is that expressed?", opts: ["@ConditionalOnWebApplication(type = Type.SERVLET) and @ConditionalOnWebApplication(type = Type.REACTIVE)", "@Profile(\"servlet\") and @Profile(\"reactive\")", "@ConditionalOnProperty(name = \"spring.main.web-application-type\")", "@ConditionalOnBean(DispatcherServlet.class)"], a: 0, e: "Web application type is a first-class condition, and it is the reliable discriminator: Type.ANY matches both, and a missing DispatcherServlet bean condition is ambiguous because bean conditions are order-sensitive. Profiles are an environment concern, not a technology discriminator, and switching on a property duplicates what the condition already knows." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_AutoConfiguration", n: "register", q: "How should a Boot 3 auto-configuration class be written, and why?", opts: ["Annotate it @AutoConfiguration (which implies proxyBeanMethods = false) and declare @Bean methods with conditions", "Annotate it @Configuration(proxyBeanMethods = true) so the conditions can call each other", "Annotate it @Component so the component scan picks it up", "Annotate it @Configuration and put it in the same package as your application"], a: 0, e: "@AutoConfiguration is the explicit marker for a candidate; proxyBeanMethods = false means @Bean methods call each other directly instead of through the enhanced configuration class, which is both faster and avoids CGLIB final-method limits. Component scanning is not how auto-configurations are found - they are loaded from the imports file - and package placement is irrelevant." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Config_Properties", n: "relaxed", q: "Which environment variable binds to spring.datasource.hikari.maximum-pool-size = 20?", opts: ["SPRING_DATASOURCE_HIKARI_MAXIMUM_POOL_SIZE=20", "SPRING_DATASOURCE.HIKARI.MAXIMUM-POOL-SIZE=20", "DATASOURCE_HIKARI_MAXIMUM_POOL_SIZE=20", "SPRING_DATASOURCE_HIKARI_MAXIMUMPOOLSIZE=20"], a: 0, e: "Relaxed binding maps the canonical kebab-case property to the uppercase, underscore-separated env form, and both are equivalent. Dots, hyphens and missing separators are not: the env name must be a clean, uppercase, underscore form of the property path. A prefix-less name like DATASOURCE_... binds to a different property entirely and is silently ignored." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Config_Properties", n: "cmdline", q: "The same property is set three ways: --server.port=9090 on the command line, SERVER_PORT=8081 in the environment, and server.port=8080 in application.properties. Which value wins?", opts: ["9090 - command line arguments outrank OS environment variables, which outrank application.properties", "8080 - application.properties is the base configuration everything else decorates", "8081 - the environment always wins because it is set outside the jar", "The application fails to start because server.port is defined more than once"], a: 0, e: "Boot's precedence list puts command line arguments above ServletConfig/ServletContext params, above JNDI, above system properties, above OS environment variables, and above both profile-specific and plain application.properties. Defining a property more than once is normal and is exactly how you override packaged defaults. The env var is the right answer only if the command line flag is absent." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Config_Properties", n: "profileprops", q: "How do you activate application-dev.properties, and what happens to the plain application.properties values?", opts: ["spring.profiles.active=dev (or SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE) overlays it on top of application.properties, overriding only the keys it defines", "application-dev.properties replaces application.properties entirely", "The dev file is loaded only in production", "It is loaded automatically whenever the file exists"], a: 0, e: "Profile-specific documents are an additional, higher-precedence source of properties, merged key by key over the non-profile-specific ones - so keys you do not override keep their default values. Profile selection is never automatic from file existence, and the more specific documents (application-dev.properties outside the jar) beat the less specific ones inside it." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Config_Properties", n: "imports", q: "Why use spring.config.import=optional:file:/etc/app/ instead of a classpath: file?", opts: ["Config data is location- and precedence-aware: imports are processed as documents that can be optional, and they let you layer external files or a config server with predictable ordering", "It is a legacy syntax kept only for Spring Cloud", "It disables profile-specific documents", "It converts properties into YAML"], a: 0, e: "The Config Data API treats every source as a document with a defined order, which is what makes external configuration (mounted secrets, a config server) predictable. optional: is the part developers miss - without it a missing file fails startup. The other options invent behaviour the mechanism does not have." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Config_Properties", n: "envenv", q: "How does a plain Environment.getProperty(\"app.region\") call resolve a value that lives in a file named APP_REGION, and what is the caveat?", opts: ["SystemEnvironmentPropertySource maps APP_REGION to app.region via relaxed name matching, so it resolves; the caveat is that the same source is not enumerable the same way and the mapping is heuristic", "It never resolves, because System.getenv is not part of the Environment", "It resolves only if the value is also declared in application.properties", "It resolves but the value is always upper-cased"], a: 0, e: "Boot's system environment property source translates relaxed names in both directions, which is why an env-only property can be read. The caveats that matter in practice: this source has no declared property names, so it cannot be listed by the configprops endpoint, and the heuristic translation means a value can be found under a name you did not write down - which is exactly why you prefer typed @ConfigurationProperties for real configuration." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Config_Properties", n: "configprops", q: "What is the difference between @ConfigurationPropertiesScan and @EnableConfigurationProperties?", opts: ["@ConfigurationPropertiesScan registers every @ConfigurationProperties class in the scanned packages; @EnableConfigurationProperties registers the specific classes you name", "@ConfigurationPropertiesScan only works in tests", "@EnableConfigurationProperties binds values from the environment while @ConfigurationPropertiesScan binds from a file", "They are interchangeable in all cases"], a: 0, e: "Both register the bean that performs binding and validation, but the scan finds types for you (including support for @ConfigurationProperties records and constructor binding) while @EnableConfigurationProperties is explicit and names the class - useful when the class lives in a library or a different package. The mapping of @ConfigurationPropertiesScan defaults to the annotated class's package, so a class elsewhere needs basePackages." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Config_Properties", n: "validation", q: "You add @Validated and @NotNull on a record field in a @ConfigurationProperties class. What happens when the property is absent?", opts: ["Startup fails with a bind/validation exception naming the property, because JSR-380 validation is applied while binding", "The field is left null and the failure appears on first use", "Validation runs only under the dev profile", "A default value of null is registered and validation passes"], a: 0, e: "Validating the configuration at startup is the point of the pattern: fail fast, with a message naming the offending property, instead of discovering a null in production. You have to put @Validated (or jakarta.validation constraints with constructor binding) on the properties class and let the registration happen via @ConfigurationPropertiesScan or @EnableConfigurationProperties; without the validation being applied, null constraints are never evaluated." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Data_Model", n: "order", q: "Order has a List<OrderItem> and OrderItem has an Order. Which side owns the relationship, and what is the practical consequence?", opts: ["OrderItem owns it (@ManyToOne with @JoinColumn, or mappedBy on the other side); changing the child collection on the wrong side silently does nothing", "Order owns it, because the parent is always the owning side", "Both own it, and the database ends up with two foreign keys", "Neither owns it; JPA infers the owning side from the @Id"], a: 0, e: "The owning side is the side that holds the foreign key, which for a parent/child pair is normally the child. @OneToMany(mappedBy = ...) is a read-only inverse view: add items to the collection without setting the child's order reference and nothing is persisted. The mapping follows the schema, not the object graph." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Data_Model", n: "book", q: "What are the JPA defaults for @ManyToOne and @OneToMany, and which one surprises people on a large result set?", opts: ["@ManyToOne defaults to EAGER and @OneToMany to LAZY; the eager many-to-one is what turns one query per parent into a join explosion", "Both default to LAZY in JPA", "Both default to EAGER, so joins are unavoidable", "Both default to LAZY, so the N+1 problem cannot happen"], a: 0, e: "The asymmetry is spec behaviour: the many side is a single reference and is loaded eagerly, the one side is a collection and is proxied. That eager fetch is why 'one query per book' degrades into a huge cartesian join with multiple collections, and why making those associations LAZY is often the first performance fix." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Data_Model", n: "orderitem", q: "OrderItem has no surrogate id and is identified by (order_id, book_id). What is the idiomatic mapping?", opts: ["@EmbeddedId with an @Embeddable key class containing both columns, or a Map key on a @OneToMany", "A @GeneratedValue id you hide from the API", "@IdClass with two @Id fields but no embedded key class", "A @Transient composite field and manual SQL in the repository"], a: 0, e: "A composite key needs a real key type: @EmbeddedId or @IdClass, so equals, hashCode and detachment behaviour are well defined. A generated surrogate id is a legitimate modelling choice if you want a stable identifier for the line, but hiding a generated key is not the same as modelling a composite key, and transient fields plus hand-written SQL lose lazy loading, flush ordering and query derivation." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Data_Model", n: "user", q: "A User entity overrides equals and hashCode using the generated id. What breaks?", opts: ["Until the entity is persisted the id is null, so it lands in a HashSet under a mutable hash - then its hash changes on flush and it disappears from the set", "Nothing; Hibernate handles null ids", "The entity cannot be used in a @OneToMany", "equals is never called for entities"], a: 0, e: "Hash-based collections require a hash that does not change, and the id is assigned at flush time. Use the business key (email, natural key) or the id only for already-persisted instances and be consistent - which is exactly what the generated equals/hashCode in project Lombok and IDEs usually gets wrong for entities in both directions." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Data_Model", n: "payment", q: "Why is @Enumerated(EnumType.STRING) the usual choice for a status column, and what happens with the default?", opts: ["It stores the constant name, so reordering or renaming constants is visible in the data; the default ordinal form silently reinterprets existing rows when the enum changes", "It is faster to query with an index", "It allows null values in a non-nullable column", "The default is more readable in the database"], a: 0, e: "String form is decoupled from declaration order, so inserting a constant in the middle cannot rewrite history, and the value in the database is readable during an incident. Ordinals are compact but the column silently changes meaning; renaming or reordering is then a data migration, not a code change. The other options are not reasons to choose either form." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Nplus1", n: "loop", q: "What exactly triggers an N+1 select problem?", opts: ["A lazy association is dereferenced inside a loop, so the persistence context issues one extra query per iteration", "The repository method is called more than once", "A @Transactional annotation is missing", "The database has no index on the foreign key"], a: 0, e: "The pattern is: one query fetches the parents, then each row triggers its own lazy load. The count is deterministic - N+1 - and the tell is SQL log volume that grows with result size. A missing index makes each of those extra queries slower but does not create them, and calling a repository twice is just two queries." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Nplus1", n: "query101", q: "You call orderRepository.findAll() and then order.getItems() for each order inside a @Transactional method. How many SQL statements do you see with lazy collections?", opts: ["1 findAll + 1 select per order - the N+1 pattern", "Exactly 1, because the persistence context batches the collection loads", "2, because Hibernate always adds a single join query", "0 additional statements, because collections are cached"], a: 0, e: "Each collection is a separate proxy that executes on first access, so the statement count scales with the result set. The persistence context caches by identity, which does not help when the rows are distinct collections, and the second-level cache is off by default. Only a join fetch or an entity graph collapses it to one statement." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Nplus1", n: "joinfetch", q: "You add join fetch to the repository query, but the response still triggers extra queries. What is the usual reason?", opts: ["A second, lazily loaded collection is accessed outside the query - one query can only fetch multiple bags safely if they are not both bag collections", "join fetch is ignored unless the method name starts with findBy", "The @Transactional annotation was removed from the service", "The query must be a native query to use fetch joins"], a: 0, e: "Fetch joins solve the problem you fetch on; a second collection - typically another LAZY bag - either fails with 'cannot simultaneously fetch multiple bags' or loads separately. Fix the remaining collection with a second query, an @EntityGraph, or map one side as a Set, and always use distinct with the join fetch. JPQL joins work fine, and @Transactional is not what makes a fetch join work." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Nplus1", n: "fixedservice", q: "Which change reduces N+1 to a small fixed number of queries without rewriting the query?", opts: ["Set hibernate.default_batch_fetch_size (or @BatchSize) so collection and entity loads are batched into IN clauses", "Add @Query(nativeQuery = true) to the same derived query", "Switch the repository to JpaRepository instead of CrudRepository", "Annotate the loop with @Transactional(propagation = REQUIRES_NEW)"], a: 0, e: "Batch fetching groups the individual loads Hibernate would have issued into IN queries, so the count becomes roughly 1 plus a couple regardless of N - at the cost of memory and a slightly larger working set. The other options change nothing: the repository base interface, the query flavour and the transaction propagation are all orthogonal to how many statements Hibernate issues." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Nplus1", n: "onequery", q: "When you fix N+1 with @EntityGraph(attributePaths = ...) or a fetch join, which other details still matter?", opts: ["You may still get duplicates, so projections need distinct; with two to-many fetches you need a Set or a two-step load, and pagination must be on the root query", "Nothing - the graph makes the result one-to-one by construction", "You must also add an index on every joined column", "You must remove the @Transactional annotation"], a: 0, e: "Fetch joins are ordinary joins, so a parent with N children appears N times and DTO construction may hand you duplicates - which is why distinct is often needed. Two to-many fetches in one query is the classic 'multiple bag fetches' error, and in-memory pagination on a fetch-joined collection is silently wrong. An index helps join cost but is unrelated to the duplicate rows." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Transactions", n: "required", q: "An outer transactional method calls an inner method also annotated REQUIRED. What is the result?", opts: ["One physical transaction: the inner call joins it, participates in the same rollback rules, and nothing commits until the outer method returns", "Two transactions, both committing independently", "No transaction, because REQUIRED only applies to the outermost call", "A read-only transaction, because propagation always downgrades"], a: 0, e: "REQUIRED means 'join if one exists, create otherwise', which is the default. The inner method's rollback marks the shared transaction rollback-only, and the commit happens once at the top - so an inner failure that you swallow still kills the outer commit. Nothing is independent and nothing is read-only." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Transactions", n: "requiresnew", q: "Why does REQUIRES_NEW require a second database connection, and what is the production risk?", opts: ["It suspends the current transaction and opens a new one, so it needs a second connection from the pool - if the pool is small, the outer transaction starves waiting for one and the app deadlocks or times out", "It copies the data to a new database", "It needs a second EntityManagerFactory", "It locks the whole table until the new transaction commits"], a: 0, e: "Suspending does not release the connection: the outer transaction keeps holding its connection while the inner one checks out another, so a pool smaller than your nesting depth self-deadlocks. Hikari's 'Connection is not available, request timed out' is the classic symptom. The risk scales with how often the code path runs, not with how often the DB is slow." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Transactions", n: "rollback", q: "Under the default rollback rules, which of these roll back a @Transactional method? (multiple may apply - pick the one that is always true)", opts: ["RuntimeException and Error; checked exceptions do NOT roll back unless you set rollbackFor", "Any Throwable, always", "Only checked exceptions", "Nothing rolls back - you must call setRollbackOnly yourself"], a: 0, e: "The default rule mirrors EJB3 and is a classic trap: throw new IOException() and your transaction commits. Declare rollbackFor = Exception.class (or unchecked = Exception.class) when the method can throw checked failures that matter. Nothing about the exception type marks the transaction automatically, and rollback-only is a manual escape hatch, not the default." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Transactions", n: "poison", q: "An inner transactional method throws, you catch it in the outer method and continue. What does the outer method see when it returns?", opts: ["UnexpectedRollbackException - the shared transaction was already marked rollback-only, and the outer attempt to commit fails", "The data commits, because the exception was handled", "Only the inner work is rolled back", "A deadlock"], a: 0, e: "The inner call joined the same transaction, so the failure poisoned it; catching the exception in application code does not un-mark it, and commit fails loudly rather than silently. Either do not catch, or use REQUIRES_NEW for the part that must survive - which is why 'I caught the exception and it still rolled back' is such a common and confusing production surprise." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Transactions", n: "nested", q: "How does NESTED propagation differ from REQUIRES_NEW?", opts: ["NESTED uses a savepoint inside the current transaction, so the inner work can roll back independently without a second transaction or connection", "NESTED opens a second transaction on a second connection", "NESTED is only supported on the reactive manager", "NESTED commits the outer transaction early"], a: 0, e: "NESTED is a savepoint: same transaction, same connection, partial rollback only. It requires the platform to issue SAVEPOINT statements, so it depends on driver and transaction manager support, and it is easy to forget it is not a real independent transaction. REQUIRES_NEW is a genuinely separate transaction on a second connection - and commits the inner work even if the outer later rolls back." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Transactions", n: "commit", q: "For a REQUIRED transaction started by a service method, when does the database actually see the commit?", opts: ["When the proxied method returns normally, the transaction manager commits - not when each statement is executed", "After each SQL statement, because auto-commit is on", "When the HTTP response is written to the socket", "When the connection is returned to the pool"], a: 0, e: "The transaction manager is what turns auto-commit off, issues the work, and commits at the boundary. Committing per statement would defeat the purpose of the unit of work, and the connection returning to the pool is a later, different event that is only safe because the transaction already ended. Writing the response happens after the commit, which is why a failed response cannot roll the data back." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Transactions", n: "outer", q: "You annotate the controller method with @Transactional instead of the service method. What breaks?", opts: ["The transaction covers the web layer - a later LazyInitializationException or an over-long transaction that holds a pooled connection across serialization and response writing", "Nothing; the boundary is wherever you put it", "Spring MVC forbids @Transactional on controllers", "The transaction commits before the repository call"], a: 0, e: "A transaction on the controller holds a pooled connection for the whole request, including view rendering and socket writes, and it spreads the persistence concern into the adapter layer where every other caller forgets it. MVC does not forbid it - which is why the mistake is silent. The commit still happens when the annotated method returns; the damage is scope, not timing." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Transactions", n: "inner", q: "A method with REQUIRES_NEW is called from a REQUIRED method, and the outer method then throws. What is the final state?", opts: ["The inner work is already committed and survives; the outer work is rolled back", "Both are rolled back", "Both commit", "The inner work is rolled back and the outer commits"], a: 0, e: "REQUIRES_NEW is a real independent transaction: it commits when it returns, so it cannot be undone by the outer failure. That is exactly why people reach for it to write audit or error records - and exactly why it is dangerous as a default, because it also commits work the caller expected to disappear, and it needs a second connection from the pool." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Caching", n: "cacheproxy", q: "Why does @Cacheable sometimes seem to do nothing when the method is called from another method in the same class?", opts: ["The cache advice lives in a proxy; a self-invocation bypasses it, so the annotation is inert on internal calls", "@Cacheable only works on methods with arguments", "The cache is empty because the first call returned null", "@Cacheable requires a public class and a no-arg constructor"], a: 0, e: "Same root cause as self-invocation with @Transactional and @Async: the annotation is honoured by the proxy, and an internal call never reaches it. Null results are cached too, and a cacheable method needs no arguments to work. Fix it by splitting the class or self-injecting the proxy - the same fix you already know from transactions." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Caching", n: "cache", q: "What does @Cacheable do when the annotated method throws?", opts: ["Nothing is cached, because the cache is only populated on successful return", "The exception is cached and rethrown on later calls", "A partial entry is written and then evicted", "The cache is flushed entirely"], a: 0, e: "The advice stores the return value on the normal path, so failures are not cached - which is usually what you want, though it also means a persistent failure keeps hammering the database unless you add a negative cache or a circuit breaker. Exceptions are not serializable values, and there is no partial write; if you need failure caching, @Cacheable(cacheManager = ...) with a wrapped result is the pattern." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Caching", n: "evictor", q: "A service updates a book and calls a @CacheEvict method - but the cache still serves stale data. What is the most common cause?", opts: ["The evict method is invoked internally (self-invocation) or through a non-proxied reference, so the eviction never runs", "Eviction happens before the update commits, so it caches the old value again", "@CacheEvict requires a transaction", "Caffeine evicts asynchronously, so the entry survives briefly"], a: 0, e: "Eviction is advice, so it has exactly the same proxy requirement as @Cacheable - a self-invoked call is a no-op. Eviction does not need a transaction, and eviction is synchronous, not deferred. A real timing bug is possible with cache-aside and concurrent writes, which is where a short TTL and write-through or versioned keys help." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Caching", n: "cachemgr", q: "Which CacheManager does Spring Boot use by default for a simple local cache, and what do you change to get a shared one?", opts: ["Caffeine (or ConcurrentMapCacheManager if Caffeine is absent); for a shared cache add a Redis starter and Boot switches to RedisCacheManager", "Always ConcurrentMapCacheManager unless you write your own", "Always RedisCacheManager, which falls back to a local map when Redis is absent", "Always EhCache, with Caffeine only in tests"], a: 0, e: "Boot auto-configures a local Caffeine cache when that library is present, and falls back to a simple ConcurrentMapCacheManager, which is unbounded and fine only for tiny, test-only caches. Boot does not silently fall back from Redis to a local map - if a Redis connection is configured and unreachable, you get a timeout per call, not a local cache - and a shared cache is the only thing that works across replicas." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Caching", n: "db", q: "You want a per-customer cache key built from an argument. Which is the idiomatic way?", opts: ["@Cacheable(key = \"#customerId\") or @Cacheable(key = \"#root.args[0].id\"), with the SpEL evaluated against the method arguments", "@Cacheable(cacheNames = customerId) - the argument name becomes the key", "@Cacheable(keyGenerator = \"uuidGenerator\") so each call gets a unique entry", "@Cacheable plus @CachePut on the same method"], a: 0, e: "SpEL key expressions are the supported mechanism: #id names a parameter, #p0/#a0 index it, and #root gives you the whole argument set, so composite keys like #customer.id + ':' + #product.id are natural. The method name is a cache name, not a key; a random key defeats the cache; and @CachePut on the same method is a confusing combination that always overwrites on read." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Caching", n: "client", q: "Two application replicas both run a local Caffeine cache behind one load balancer. What is the user-visible symptom?", opts: ["Stale data that depends on which replica answers - each replica has its own cache and its own eviction timing", "Cache misses on every request", "OutOfMemory, because Caffeine cannot be shared", "Consistent data, because the cache is keyed by the method"], a: 0, e: "Local caches are per-JVM, so reads are non-deterministic across replicas and evictions only reach the instance that performed the write - this is the classic 'works on my instance' bug. You either move to a shared cache manager (Redis) or accept staleness and shrink the TTL. Caffeine and keying have nothing to do with the memory or miss symptoms described." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Caching", n: "service", q: "You enable @EnableCaching and @Cacheable on a service method, then hit the endpoint twice. What is the expected SQL pattern?", opts: ["First call runs the query and populates the cache; the second call serves the cached value and issues no SQL, until eviction or TTL expiry", "Both calls run the query, because the transaction manager bypasses the cache", "The first call returns cached data from a previous run, the second queries", "The cache only works for write methods"], a: 0, e: "That is the cache-aside pattern: a miss populates, a hit does not touch the database, and eviction or TTL expiry returns you to the database. The transaction manager is orthogonal, and the first call in a fresh process is always a miss. The interesting production detail is what invalidates the entry - evict on write, or accept a short TTL." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Caching", n: "controller", q: "Should you annotate a @RestController method with @Cacheable? What are the trade-offs?", opts: ["It works and covers the serialized response, but it caches presentation concerns, hides per-user variation unless your key includes identity, and skips service-level reuse for internal callers", "No, it is forbidden on controllers", "Yes, and it is always better because it avoids serializing twice", "No, because controllers are proxied and @Cacheable cannot apply"], a: 0, e: "Controllers are proxied beans, so the annotation does apply - which is exactly the problem: you are caching a wire representation, you must build a correct key (customer, locale, roles) yourself, and any internal caller of the service bypasses the cache. The correct default is to cache the expensive domain operation in the service and let the same cache serve web and batch callers." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Security_JWT", n: "chain", q: "In a default Spring Security Boot application, where does authentication happen and what is the shape of the chain?", opts: ["A FilterChainProxy holds an ordered list of security filters; the authentication filters run early and populate a SecurityContext for the request", "Inside the controller, via an @AuthenticationPrincipal resolver", "In a servlet container valve configured in server.xml", "Inside the DispatcherServlet's HandlerAdapter"], a: 0, e: "Spring Security is a filter chain, and the default Boot order starts with CSRF, then authentication, then authorization, then the exception-translation filters that turn failures into 401 or 403. The @AuthenticationPrincipal resolver only reads what the filters already stored, so if the chain does not run - a filter ordering mistake, or a URL you excluded - nothing downstream can authenticate anyone." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Security_JWT", n: "filter", q: "Where in the request does a stateless JWT filter belong, and what must it do on failure?", opts: ["Early in the chain, before authorization; on an invalid or expired token it must hand off to the AuthenticationEntryPoint so the client gets 401, not a bare 403", "After the controller, as a response wrapper", "Inside the service layer, by parsing the Authorization header manually", "Only on /login"], a: 0, e: "The token filter must run before authorization so the SecurityContext is populated, and the correct failure behaviour is delegation to the entry point - unauthenticated means 401, authenticated-but-denied means 403, and conflating them breaks client retry logic. Doing it in the service layer is the hand-rolled approach that misses every endpoint you forgot." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Security_JWT", n: "ctx", q: "What is the role of SecurityContextHolder in a stateless API?", opts: ["A per-thread store of the current Authentication for the request; the filters set it on the way in and the framework clears it on the way out, so nothing persists in a session", "It stores JWTs in the HTTP session so they survive restarts", "It is a cache of validated tokens keyed by subject", "It holds the CSRF token"], a: 0, e: "SecurityContextHolder is a thread-local strategy: with SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS no session is created, so each request re-authenticates from the bearer token. That is also why a filter that forgets to clear the context, or an error dispatch, can leak an authentication into another request. CSRF tokens and token caches are unrelated mechanisms." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Security_JWT", n: "entrypoint", q: "A client gets 403 when it sends no Authorization header, and 403 when it sends a valid token lacking the required role. What should be configured?", opts: ["A custom AuthenticationEntryPoint returning 401 for the anonymous case, and an AccessDeniedHandler returning 403 for the denied case", "Both should return 401, since the token is involved", "Both should return 302 to a login page", "Neither - the controller should throw ResponseStatusException"], a: 0, e: "ExceptionTranslationFilter picks the entry point when the Authentication is anonymous or missing and the access-denied handler when a real Authentication lacks the authority, so the two situations are distinguishable and should be reported differently. Spring's defaults are 302 (form login) or 403 for both, which is why REST APIs configure both handlers explicitly. @PreAuthorize denials arrive as AccessDeniedException and are handled by the denial handler." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Security_JWT", n: "client", q: "For a resource server that validates JWTs issued elsewhere, what do you configure in Boot 3?", opts: ["spring-boot-starter-oauth2-resource-server with a jwk-set-uri (or a public-key location), plus a converter if you need roles from claims", "spring-boot-starter-security plus a hand-written filter that parses the token", "A UserDetailsService with a hard-coded token list", "Session cookies with rememberMe()"], a: 0, e: "The resource-server starter supplies the standard chain, Nimbus-based validation of signature, issuer and expiry, and key retrieval from the issuer's JWKS. A hand-written filter is legitimate when you need control, but then key rotation, clock skew and claim mapping are yours to own. Session cookies and UserDetailsService are the stateful alternative and mean the token is not validated per request at all." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Observability", n: "actuator", q: "After adding spring-boot-starter-actuator, what is exposed over HTTP by default, and how do you change it?", opts: ["Only /actuator/health and /actuator/info over the web; anything else needs management.endpoints.web.exposure.include", "All endpoints, always", "No endpoints until management.endpoints.enabled-by-default is set", "Only /actuator/metrics"], a: 0, e: "Actuator deliberately ships a small, safe web surface - health and info - and exposing metrics, env, configprops, loggers, heapdump or shutdown is an explicit decision you make. enabled-by-default is a different switch: it controls whether endpoint beans exist at all, which is what you use on non-web deployments. Getting this wrong in either direction is common: a fleet-wide scrape failing, or an env endpoint leaking secrets." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Observability", n: "health", q: "What does the default /actuator/health aggregate, and why is that a deployment concern?", opts: ["A composite status from every registered HealthIndicator - disk space, db, and anything custom; a DOWN result fails a readiness probe and pulls the pod from the Service", "Only the application's own up/down flag", "Only the database", "Nothing unless you write a HealthIndicator"], a: 0, e: "Boot auto-registers indicators for the technologies on your classpath, and the overall status is the worst of them - which is exactly what a readiness gate consumes. That is how a shared external system can take an otherwise healthy pod out of rotation, and the reason production setups split health into groups instead of one flat status." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Observability", n: "liveness", q: "What should a liveness probe check, and what happens if you let it check the database?", opts: ["Only that the process can make progress, typically nothing more than 'the context is up'; a DB check makes every pod restart during a database blip, turning a dependency outage into a full outage", "Database connectivity, so a restart fixes stale connections", "Full readiness, so the probe is maximally strict", "Disk usage only"], a: 0, e: "Liveness answers 'should this process be killed and replaced', so everything it checks must be fixable by restarting the process. Readiness answers 'should this process get traffic' and may check dependencies. Kubernetes enables the probes group automatically, which is why an unguarded liveness check silently couples your pod lifecycle to your database's." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Observability", n: "readiness", q: "How do you make readiness wait for the database and for warm-up, without dragging liveness along?", opts: ["Enable the probes group and define health groups: readiness includes readinessState plus db, liveness includes only livenessState", "Use the same health group for both probes", "Add a sleep before the liveness endpoint answers", "Disable the actuator and rely on fixed deploy delays"], a: 0, e: "The probes group is created automatically on Kubernetes, and health groups let you say readiness = started and the DB answers, while liveness = the JVM is not stuck. Reusing one group for both re-creates the DB-in-liveness bug, and a sleep in the probe is a race with extra steps that gets worse on a busy deploy." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Observability", n: "dbhealth", q: "You run Flyway migrations on application startup with several replicas. What goes wrong and what is the robust design?", opts: ["Replicas race on DDL locks and one pod's migration can break another's; run migrations as a separate job or init step, then gate readiness on schema readiness", "Nothing - Flyway takes a lock and serialises migrations automatically", "Migration failure only affects the pod that ran it", "Flyway is only for SQL Server"], a: 0, e: "Flyway (and Liquibase) do lock the history table, so concurrent startups queue up - but that turns pod startup order into a deployment dependency and a slow migration chain can exceed the pod's startup budget. The robust design separates schema change from application start: a migration job in the pipeline, then readiness gated on the database the app actually needs. Option 1 is wrong because a migration error usually means every pod fails identically." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Observability", n: "micrometer", q: "How does a metric recorded with Micrometer in Spring Boot reach Prometheus?", opts: ["Add micrometer-registry-prometheus; the MeterRegistry records it, the actuator exposes the scrape endpoint, and Prometheus pulls from it", "The JVM pushes metrics to a remote endpoint automatically", "Actuator POSTs metrics to Datadog once the starter is present", "Only built-in HTTP metrics are recorded unless you add an exporter"], a: 0, e: "Boot configures a MeterRegistry from whichever registry implementation it finds, the actuator serves Prometheus/OpenMetrics text at /actuator/prometheus, and scraping is pull-based. Web, JVM, Hikari pool and cache metrics come for free once a registry is on the classpath; the remaining work is naming your meters and tags carefully, since cardinality is where metrics platforms fall over." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Observability", n: "otel", q: "Which pieces do you need to get distributed traces out of a Spring Boot 3 service, and what is Micrometer's role?", opts: ["An OpenTelemetry SDK plus an OTLP exporter, bridged through Micrometer Tracing; Micrometer provides the vendor-neutral Observation API and manages the tracer and context propagation", "Nothing extra - @Transactional alone produces spans", "Only a logback pattern including %X{traceId}", "A vendor agent attached via JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS"], a: 0, e: "Micrometer supplies Observation and the instrumenting API so that Spring MVC, the scheduler and the repository produce consistent signals, and Micrometer Tracing manages the tracer lifecycle; the OTel SDK and exporter are what ship the spans. Log correlation is a poor substitute - it gives you identifiers without timing or causality - and the agent is one transport option, not the only one." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Observability", n: "logs", q: "How do you correlate a log line with a distributed trace, and why does it matter operationally?", opts: ["Include the traceId and spanId in the pattern (or use structured JSON logging with a correlation field) so an alert links straight to the trace; MDC only works if context propagation is configured for async and remote hops", "Set logging.level.root=DEBUG and search for the timestamp", "Include the request URL in every message", "Enable Actuator metrics and correlate by pod name"], a: 0, e: "Trace and span identifiers are what turn a stack of log lines into a story, and they must be carried in MDC and propagated to worker threads and remote services. Timestamps are ambiguous under concurrency, a URL is not unique per request, and pod name tells you where, not which call - this is the difference between a five-minute and a two-hour incident." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Async_Scheduling", n: "proxy", q: "Why does an @Async method sometimes appear to run synchronously?", opts: ["It is invoked from inside the same class, so the call bypasses the async proxy and runs on the caller's thread; private and final methods cannot be advised either", "Async only works on classes annotated @Service", "Async requires @EnableAsync on the calling class", "Async only works for void methods"], a: 0, e: "@Async is advice on a proxy, so internal calls never reach it - the tell is that the work is fast and the caller's thread was busy for the whole duration. @EnableAsync belongs on a configuration class, and CompletableFuture or Future return values are supported as well as void. The fix is the same self-invocation workaround you already know from transactions." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Async_Scheduling", n: "executor", q: "You add @Async with no custom executor. What actually runs the task, and what is the risk under load?", opts: ["A SimpleAsyncTaskExecutor that creates a thread per task with no bound, so bursts turn into memory pressure or thread exhaustion", "A fixed pool sized to the number of CPUs", "The Tomcat request thread pool, so the endpoint still blocks", "A single-threaded scheduler shared with @Scheduled"], a: 0, e: "Boot only auto-configures a TaskExecutor for @Async when the application provides one (a bean named taskExecutor, or any Executor), otherwise the uncapped default is used. A bounded pool with a bounded queue and an explicit rejection policy is the difference between shedding load and an outage. Nothing runs on the request thread by default, and single-threaded behaviour belongs to @Scheduled." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Async_Scheduling", n: "taskdecorator", q: "An async task logs a different traceId and the current user is null. What is the fix?", opts: ["Add a TaskDecorator (or Micrometer context propagation with a ContextSnapshot) that copies MDC, the SecurityContext and RequestContextHolder into the worker thread and clears them afterwards", "Switch to @Async(proxyMode = DIRECT) so it runs on the caller thread", "Set spring.jpa.open-in-view=false", "Catch and rethrow the exception inside the task"], a: 0, e: "Thread-locals do not follow you to another thread: a TaskDecorator is the supported hook to capture and replay MDC, the SecurityContext and the servlet request attributes, and it must clean up so pooled threads do not leak state into the next task. DIRECT mode makes execution synchronous, which is exactly what you want in tests, not in production. Open-in-view and exception handling are unrelated." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Async_Scheduling", n: "scheduler", q: "What does @Scheduled use by default, and what changes if you define a TaskScheduler bean?", opts: ["A single-threaded scheduler, so one slow job delays every other job; defining a TaskScheduler bean (or spring.task.scheduling.pool.size) replaces it with a pool", "A pool sized to the CPU count, always", "The @Async executor, always", "A new JVM per job"], a: 0, e: "The default is a one-thread scheduler, which is why 'my cron ran late' is such a common report and why a blocking job silently postpones everything else. Supplying a TaskScheduler gives you a pool and enables CronTrigger, FixedDelayTrigger and the reactive variant per method. @Async is a separate execution path that shares nothing with the scheduler except the possibility of a shared bean." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Async_Scheduling", n: "lock", q: "Three replicas each run a @Scheduled(cron) job that charges cards. What is the production-safe design?", opts: ["A distributed lock (ShedLock on a shared store, or a single elected consumer) plus an idempotency key, so the job runs once per schedule even across restarts and clock skew", "Nothing - the scheduler coordinates replicas", "Use fixedDelay instead of cron so replicas stagger", "Run the job only on the pod with the lowest IP"], a: 0, e: "Kubernetes schedules nothing: every replica fires the same cron on its own clock, so you get three charges. A lock prevents overlap; idempotency handles the harder case where the lock holder dies after doing the work but before recording it. Neither fixedDelay nor a naming convention solves it - the alternative is a dedicated worker deployment or a leader-elected consumer." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Messaging", n: "outbox", q: "Why write the message into an outbox table in the same transaction as the order instead of publishing from @Transactional code?", opts: ["Because the data change and the message must commit atomically - publishing inside the transaction can send before the commit (consumer sees nothing) or after a rollback (ghost event)", "Because brokers cannot be called inside a transaction", "Because JPA forbids network calls within a transaction", "Because the outbox is faster than direct publishing"], a: 0, e: "This is the dual-write problem: you cannot atomically commit to a database and to a broker. The outbox turns the business change plus an intent record into one atomic commit, and a separate relay publishes and marks rows as sent. The relay is at-least-once, so consumers must be idempotent - the outbox relocates the failure rather than abolishing it." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Messaging", n: "relay", q: "What does the outbox relay need to get right, and what does it trade away?", opts: ["Publish at least once (publish then mark, or mark then publish), with retries, backoff and a dead-letter path; the cost is possible duplicates and a small publish delay", "Exactly-once delivery, guaranteed by the database commit", "No duplicates, because rows are deleted after publishing", "Immediate delivery, because it reuses the business transaction's connection"], a: 0, e: "Any ordering of 'publish' and 'mark sent' leaves a failure window, so the honest guarantee is at-least-once plus idempotent consumers, with visibility-timeout-style retries and alerting on the oldest unsent row. Exactly-once across a database and a broker is not something a commit can provide. The relay is deliberately a separate step, which also gives you a natural place to add tracing and backpressure." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Messaging", n: "consumer", q: "A consumer receives the same order.created event twice after a relay retry. What makes the handler safe?", opts: ["An idempotency key: a unique constraint on the message id (or a processed-messages table) written in the same transaction as the effect, so the second delivery is a no-op", "Manual acknowledgement with autoAck = true", "Making the listener method readOnly", "Raising the broker's max poll interval"], a: 0, e: "Under at-least-once delivery, dedupe has to be enforced where the effect happens, atomically with it - a unique insert that fails means 'already done'. A business key such as the order id is often better than a transport id, because a redelivery may carry a new message id. Auto-acknowledge makes things worse, and readOnly concerns transaction semantics, not duplicates." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Messaging", n: "sideeffect", q: "A listener makes a slow HTTP call and then updates state. Which design detail deserves the most scrutiny?", opts: ["When the offset is acknowledged: long work in the listener holds the partition, so retries and rebalancing stall - take a pointer, ack, and do the work asynchronously, or split into a fast handler plus a queue", "The JSON serializer, because slow calls always mean a bad serializer", "The topic's partition count, which must equal the consumer thread count", "Whether the listener is @Transactional"], a: 0, e: "Consumer lag is a function of how long you hold the offset, so an external call inside the listener is the classic throughput ceiling. Partitions and threading are real tuning concerns but not the cause of stalled progress. @Transactional on a listener is legitimate and only gives atomic work-plus-offset when the container supports transaction-aware acknowledgement; otherwise you are at-least-once again." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Deployment", n: "image", q: "Why ship a layered jar or an exploded jar on a distroless base, rather than one big fat image?", opts: ["The layered layout (BOOT-INF/classes plus BOOT-INF/lib) caches dependency layers across builds, so a code-only change pushes kilobytes instead of the whole jar; bootBuildImage produces an OCI image with those layers", "Layered jars start faster because the JVM skips verification", "Fat images cannot run on Kubernetes", "Layered jars remove the need for a JRE"], a: 0, e: "The layering exists for build caching and registry push size, which is where deploy time actually goes. The JVM still verifies, fat images run fine on Kubernetes, and you still need a JVM. For containers the usual next step is an exploded or layered jar on a minimal, non-root base image." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Deployment", n: "jvm", q: "What container-aware JVM sizing avoids being OOM-killed on a memory-limited pod?", opts: ["-XX:MaxRAMPercentage (with headroom for metaspace, code cache and direct buffers) so the heap scales with the cgroup limit, and a limit larger than the heap plus native memory", "-Xmx equal to the pod's memory limit", "-Xmx fixed at 8g regardless of the limit", "Nothing - the JVM sizes the heap from the container limit on its own"], a: 0, e: "MaxRAMPercentage is the intended mechanism under a cgroup limit, and native memory sits outside the heap - which is why a 512Mi limit with a 400Mi heap still gets killed. Setting -Xmx to the whole limit is the classic mistake. Exit code 137 with no Java OutOfMemoryError almost always means the cgroup limit was hit before the JVM could report a Java heap error." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Deployment", n: "probes", q: "A new pod warms a cache for 90 seconds and is killed at 40. Which fix is right, and what remains necessary?", opts: ["Add a startup probe (or raise its failureThreshold) so liveness is not evaluated until startup finishes; readiness must still stay false until warm-up completes", "Raise the liveness initialDelaySeconds to 90 and delete readiness", "Disable liveness permanently", "Sleep in the application's main method"], a: 0, e: "The startup probe exists for slow starts: while it is failing, liveness and readiness are not evaluated, and the pod gets a generous budget. Readiness still has to gate traffic separately, because 'started' is not 'ready'. Sleeping in main hides the problem from the orchestrator and slows every deploy, including the good ones." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Deployment", n: "graceful", q: "Which settings stop a rolling update from dropping in-flight requests, and what does the orchestrator need on top?", opts: ["server.shutdown=graceful with spring.lifecycle.timeout-per-shutdown-phase, plus a preStop sleep and a terminationGracePeriodSeconds longer than your longest request", "Increasing the replica count", "Setting readiness to false on shutdown", "Nothing - Kubernetes already drains connections"], a: 0, e: "Graceful shutdown stops accepting new requests and lets in-flight ones finish; the preStop sleep matters because endpoint removal and pod termination are not simultaneous, and the grace period must exceed your slowest request. Scaling up first narrows the window, and readiness-to-false alone still races the kill signal." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Deployment", n: "configsource", q: "How should a containerised Spring Boot app receive per-environment configuration?", opts: ["At runtime, as environment variables or mounted files, so one immutable image is promoted through environments; build-time baking turns any rollback into a rebuild", "Baked into application-prod.properties during the Docker build", "Hard-coded defaults in @ConfigurationProperties classes only", "Fetched from a mutable git branch at startup and never pinned"], a: 0, e: "One immutable image promoted through environments is the foundation of a rollback you can actually perform, and runtime configuration is the only thing that varies per environment. Baked properties make a rollback a rebuild and redeploy of everything, and no validation means a missing variable shows up in production. Pin what you depend on, and validate configuration at startup so the failure is immediate." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Deployment", n: "registry", q: "What is wrong with tagging your container image as latest and letting every environment pull whatever is newest?", opts: ["Deploys become non-reproducible and rollbacks impossible - you cannot name the version that is running; use immutable tags or digests", "Nothing, as long as the image is built in CI", "latest forces the registry to rebuild the image", "latest prevents layer caching"], a: 0, e: "The tag becomes the only piece of identity you have, and 'latest' is a moving pointer, so two pods of the same deployment can run different code and a rollback has no target. Immutable tags plus a digest reference, and promotion by digest through environments, are the standard answer. Layer caching is unrelated to the tag's meaning, and CI-built images still need identity." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Testing_Slices", n: "unit", q: "What does a plain JUnit + Mockito test give you that a Spring test does not?", opts: ["Speed and isolation - no context, no auto-configuration, no database; the tradeoff is that it verifies interactions, not wiring or real behaviour", "Coverage of auto-configuration and the filter chain", "Real SQL against a real schema", "Automatic transaction rollback between tests"], a: 0, e: "A unit test is fast because it skips the container, but that is also its limit: it cannot catch a missing bean, a bad property or a broken query. It also tempts you into asserting mock interactions, which pins implementation rather than behaviour. The rule of thumb: test behaviour, mock at your own boundaries." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Testing_Slices", n: "webmvc", q: "What does @WebMvcTest actually load, and what must you provide?", opts: ["Only the web slice - @Controller, @ControllerAdvice, filters, WebMvcConfigurer - with other beans supplied as mocks (for example @MockitoBean) and no database", "The whole context including repositories and a real datasource", "Nothing - a plain unit test with a mocked MVC layer", "Only Jackson, with the dispatcher excluded"], a: 0, e: "The slice limits scanning to web-layer annotations and auto-configures MVC infrastructure, so a request exercises real handler mapping, validation, serialization and exception handling in milliseconds - while every collaborator is yours to supply. That combination is the point: it catches broken mappings and wrong status codes that a full context test only finds slowly. Everything below the web layer, including transaction behaviour, is out of scope." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Testing_Slices", n: "datajpa", q: "What does @DataJpaTest give you by default, and what will surprise you?", opts: ["A JPA slice with an embedded database, auto-configured repositories and a test transaction that rolls back - so propagation behaves differently than in production and H2 is not your real engine", "A real MySQL container running your production dialect", "No transaction, so each save commits immediately", "The full application context including your services"], a: 0, e: "It is a deliberately narrow slice: repositories, EntityManager and an embedded DB, with rollback between tests. Two surprises follow - dialect-specific SQL is never executed, and a test that opens its own transaction (REQUIRES_NEW, or TestTransaction) commits data the rollback never undoes, so tests interfere. Use Testcontainers for what only a real database can tell you." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Testing_Slices", n: "testcontainers", q: "What does a Testcontainers-managed database buy you over @DataJpaTest with H2?", opts: ["The real engine, dialect, collation, locking and migration behaviour, so a test that passes cannot hide a dialect-only failure until production", "Much faster tests, because containers start in milliseconds", "Automatic rollback between tests", "No need for a test profile"], a: 0, e: "Fidelity is the entire point: H2 in PostgreSQL compatibility mode is not PostgreSQL, and that gap is exactly where production-only failures live. With @ServiceConnection Boot wires the datasource URL for you, and containers are slower, not faster - so they belong in a small integration suite. Rollback still comes from your own @Transactional test." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_AntiPatterns", n: "fieldinj", q: "Why is field injection with @Autowired a production liability?", opts: ["Dependencies are invisible in the signature, the object can be constructed with nulls, and testing needs a container or reflection instead of a plain new", "Field injection is not transactional", "Field injection prevents final fields", "Field injection breaks JSON serialization"], a: 0, e: "Constructor injection makes the dependency list part of the type, keeps fields final, and lets a unit test call new OrderService(repo) with no Spring at all. Field injection is also invisible to refactoring - you can add a dependency without touching a signature, so nobody reviews it. Transactions, Jackson and final fields are all red herrings here." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_AntiPatterns", n: "checkedtx", q: "A @Transactional method throws a checked exception which you catch and log. What happens to the data?", opts: ["Nothing rolls back - the default rule only rolls back RuntimeException and Error, so partial work commits", "Everything rolls back, because the exception was logged", "Only statements after the exception roll back", "The transaction is suspended and retried"], a: 0, e: "The default rollback rule surprises people in production: a business failure expressed as a checked exception commits whatever succeeded. Annotate with rollbackFor = Exception.class, or use unchecked exceptions, and treat any swallowed exception inside a transaction as a review smell. Logging does not change transaction state, and there is no statement-level partial rollback." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_AntiPatterns", n: "eagermany", q: "You make a @ManyToOne LAZY to fix a slow query and things get worse. What happened?", opts: ["The association is now loaded per row - often inside a loop or during serialisation - so a big join became an N+1 pattern", "LAZY associations cannot be loaded, so the query is retried forever", "LAZY requires an index, and adding it invalidated the plan", "Hibernate disabled the query cache"], a: 0, e: "LAZY is not a performance setting; it defers loading, and whoever dereferences it pays - usually once per row, sometimes after the session closed. The fix is to fetch the shape you actually need with a join fetch, an entity graph or a projection into a DTO. Retries, plan invalidation and the query cache are not the mechanism." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_AntiPatterns", n: "longtx", q: "Why is a long-running @Transactional method dangerous in a service that also accepts uploads?", opts: ["It holds a pooled connection and row locks for the whole call - connection exhaustion, lock waits and replication lag; keep transactions short and do slow I/O outside them", "Long transactions are unsupported by JPA", "Long transactions disable the second-level cache", "Long transactions cannot be rolled back"], a: 0, e: "The transaction is the unit of atomicity, not the unit of work: everything inside it holds locks and a connection. A three-minute upload inside @Transactional means twenty concurrent uploads exhaust a pool of ten while every other request queues behind the lock. Do the I/O outside, wrap only the state change, and use compensation for the rest." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_AntiPatterns", n: "notimeout", q: "A RestClient call to a partner service hangs and the whole service falls over. Which single change would have contained it?", opts: ["Explicit connect and read timeouts on the HTTP client, so the call fails fast and the thread returns to the pool", "A larger Tomcat thread pool", "More aggressive retries", "Making the call @Async"], a: 0, e: "Without timeouts a slow partner pins request threads indefinitely, and a small pool turns one dependency into a total outage. Timeouts plus a bounded retry budget, a circuit breaker and a bulkhead are the standard containment set. More threads just delays exhaustion, and async moves the problem to a pool you also have to bound." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_AntiPatterns", n: "catchall", q: "A @RestControllerAdvice handles Exception.class and returns 500 with a generic message. What does it cost you?", opts: ["Every failure becomes an indistinguishable 500 - validation loses its 400, missing data loses its 404, and if the cause is not logged you have thrown away the only clue", "Nothing, as long as the message is generic", "It converts all errors into 200 with an error field", "Spring logs every exception twice"], a: 0, e: "The catch-all is what you add on day one and regret later: it flattens the status-code contract clients depend on, and swallowing the stack trace removes the only diagnostic you will get. Handle the specific exceptions, map them to meaningful statuses, log the unexpected ones at ERROR with the cause, and keep the catch-all for truly exceptional cases." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_WebFlux", n: "mono", q: "What is the difference between Mono and Flux, and which do you use for a single entity lookup?", opts: ["Mono emits zero or one item and Flux zero or more; a single lookup returns Mono, and 'no result' is an empty Mono, not null", "Mono is single-threaded and Flux is multi-threaded", "Flux is blocking and Mono is non-blocking", "Mono is for Java and Flux is for Kotlin"], a: 0, e: "Mono and Flux are about cardinality, not threading - the threading framing is a persistent source of confusion in review. Model emptiness explicitly (empty Mono, or justOrEmpty) because a null inside a stream surfaces as a confusing NullPointerException, and remember the operator chain still runs on one thread unless you move it." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_WebFlux", n: "webmvc", q: "Both spring-boot-starter-web and spring-boot-starter-webflux end up on the classpath. What does Boot do, and how do you choose?", opts: ["WebApplicationType resolves to SERVLET when both are present, so the app silently runs as MVC; exclude the starter you do not want, or set spring.main.web-application-type", "Startup fails because the two stacks are incompatible", "It picks reactive and logs a warning", "It starts two servers, one per stack"], a: 0, e: "Deduction prefers SERVLET whenever the servlet classes are present, so a stray web starter - often pulled in transitively by a reporting or admin dependency - quietly converts your reactive app into a blocking one with no error at all. Excluding the unwanted starter is best, because then the classpath tells the truth about what you run." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_WebFlux", n: "r2dbc", q: "What does R2DBC give you over JDBC, and what does it not give you?", opts: ["A non-blocking Reactive Streams driver with a connection pool and backpressure, but no JPA, no second-level cache and no bytecode-enhanced lazy loading", "The full JPA feature set including entity graphs and detached entities", "Automatic batching identical to Hibernate's", "Transparent blocking, so JPA repositories can be called from it"], a: 0, e: "R2DBC is a driver-level API: repositories, transactions and object mapping are separate concerns you choose, and Hibernate's mature features have no equivalent. Backpressure is real, and so are the trade-offs - which is why many systems keep JDBC for the write-heavy core and adopt R2DBC where concurrency per connection dominates." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_WebFlux", n: "db", q: "In a WebFlux app, what happens when a handler calls a blocking JPA repository, and what is the fix?", opts: ["It blocks an event-loop thread, capping throughput at roughly the loop's CPU count; the fix is R2DBC, or isolating the call on boundedElastic with a bounded pool", "Spring adapts it to a reactive stream automatically", "It throws UnsupportedOperationException at startup", "Each call gets its own unbounded thread"], a: 0, e: "Reactor Netty's event loops are few by design, so a few blocking calls starve the whole server - the symptom is high latency at modest CPU, not high CPU. R2DBC is the real fix; the bounded-elastic bridge is containment whose pool you must size, and virtual threads only help if the whole stack, including the pool, is sized for it." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Migration_3", n: "java", q: "What is the baseline JDK for Spring Boot 3.x, and what happens on an older one?", opts: ["Java 17; running on 11 fails with class file version errors because the platform itself requires 17", "Java 11, the same as Boot 2.7", "Java 8, because Jakarta EE 9 supports it", "Java 21, because Boot 3 requires virtual threads"], a: 0, e: "Spring Framework 6 and Boot 3 require Java 17 as a baseline; Java 21 is supported and gives you virtual threads, but it is not required. Running Boot 3 on 8 or 11 fails at class file version or against Spring's own bytecode, and no target flag makes an old JVM valid." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Migration_3", n: "namespace", q: "What is the most visible breaking change in Spring Boot 3, and what does the compiler do about it?", opts: ["javax.* to jakarta.* across persistence, validation, servlet and annotation packages; the old classes are gone, so you get cannot-find-symbol errors until every import and dependency is migrated", "Only group IDs change, so imports still compile", "jakarta.* is optional and javax.* keeps working behind a flag", "Nothing changes; javax and jakarta coexist in Boot 3"], a: 0, e: "It is a namespace migration, which is why the compiler is your checklist: every javax.persistence, javax.validation, javax.servlet and javax.annotation import has to move, and so does every third-party library that has not been rebuilt. The asymmetric trap is real - Jakarta EE 9 kept the javax namespace, so a half-migrated stack can compile against the wrong generation of EE APIs." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Migration_3", n: "props", q: "A Boot 2 application sets spring.redis.host. What happens on Boot 3?", opts: ["The property no longer binds to anything - the data starter moved to spring.data.redis.* - so it is silently ignored and the client falls back to its default host", "The property is renamed automatically at runtime", "Startup fails with a helpful message about the old key", "The property still works with a deprecation warning"], a: 0, e: "Silently ignored is the dangerous part: the app starts and the failure appears later as a connection refused in the cache layer. This is the general shape of a Boot 2 to 3 migration - few hard errors for old keys, so the safe approach is to diff the effective configuration between versions using the configprops endpoint or an environment dump, and to enable strict binding while you do it." },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Nplus1", n: "loop", q: "The Book Store has 8 books. The naive catalog endpoint runs findAll() and then reads the author and categories of each book. How many SQL statements does the endpoint actually execute, and why is the count not 8?", opts: ["18 - one findAll, then two lazy associations per book, plus the count query","8 - one query per book, which is what the loop suggests","1 - Hibernate batches every lazy proxy into the original findAll","18 books - Hibernate invents a row per association"], a: 0, e: "Measured, not guessed: the endpoint reports sqlQueries itself, because QueryCounter reads Hibernate statistics around the whole method. It answers 18 for 8 books. The count is not 8 because each lazy association access leaves the persistence context and issues its own round trip, so the cost is 1 + 2N rather than N.", ev: "nplus1", evk: "naive", t: { slug: "nplus1", step: 3 }, p: { file: "src/main/java/com/example/bookstore/service/CatalogService.java", from: 46, to: 50, note: "Two lazy accessors per book: getBookCategories() and getAuthor(). Each one can trigger queries." } },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Nplus1", n: "onequery", q: "The join-fetch endpoint returns a byte-identical payload to the naive one and costs 1 query instead of 18. What actually changed?", opts: ["The fetch plan: one query now loads author, bookCategories and category together","The loop was rewritten to batch books before mapping them","The entities were cached between the two requests","The transaction was removed so Hibernate skipped the associations"], a: 0, e: "The captured responses prove the payloads are identical, so nothing about the mapping changed. The loop in CatalogFetchService is character-for-character the same as in CatalogService. Only the repository query changed: findAllWithAuthorAndCategories left-joins the graph, so the loop has nothing left to load lazily.", ev: "nplus1", evk: "joinFetch", t: { slug: "nplus1", step: 5 }, p: { file: "src/main/java/com/example/bookstore/service/CatalogFetchService.java", from: 29, to: 33, note: "Same loop as the naive path, but the query already fetched everything." } },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Nplus1", n: "fixedservice", q: "A colleague removes the loop over books and gets 1 query. Which claim is actually correct?", opts: ["The loop was never the bug - N+1 is a fetch-plan problem, and any loop over eagerly fetched data is free","Loops and lazy loading are both wrong and should always be avoided","Loops are fine, but only inside @Transactional(readOnly = true)","Loops are the cause, and the fix is to use an EntityManager directly"], a: 0, e: "This is the idea worth keeping: the identical loop costs 2N extra queries in one service and zero in the other. The loop is not the defect, the fetch plan is. Chasing the loop leads you to rewrite working code and still miss the real cost.", t: { slug: "nplus1", step: 6 } },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Transactions", n: "inner", q: "placeOrder calls applyOrderDiscount, and applyOrderDiscount is annotated @Transactional. The runtime report lists it under bypassedMethods, not advisedMethods. Why?", opts: ["It is a self-invocation, so the call never leaves the object and the proxy is never consulted","@Transactional is ignored on methods that return a value","The method is private, so Spring cannot see it","Advice runs but is not reported for same-class calls"], a: 0, e: "The measured report is unambiguous: advisedMethods contains placeOrder, reserveCopy and recordOrderPlaced, and bypassedMethods contains OrderService.applyOrderDiscount. reserveCopy gets advice because it is a different bean and the call crosses a proxy. applyOrderDiscount stays inside the same object, so nothing intercepts it. The annotation is present and completely inert.", ev: "transactions", evk: "required", t: { slug: "transaction", step: 2 }, p: { file: "src/main/java/com/example/bookstore/service/OrderService.java", from: 99, to: 115, note: "The call at line 99 and the annotated method at 111-115. The annotation looks right; the call never crosses a proxy." } },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Transactions", n: "poison", q: "In poison mode an inner transaction throws, the catch block swallows the exception, and the method returns normally. The caller still gets HTTP 409. What went wrong?", opts: ["The inner transaction marked the outer one rollback-only, and that mark cannot be cleared - so the commit fails after the method returned","The catch block re-threw the exception under a different type","The order was rejected by a validation rule before any transaction opened","The outbox row could not be written, so the whole request was retried"], a: 0, e: "This is the trap that makes transaction bugs hard to read: the stack trace points at UnexpectedRollbackException, which the method never throws. Catching the original exception does not undo the rollback-only flag the inner transaction set on the shared transaction. The failure surfaces at commit time, far from the line that caused it.", ev: "transactions", evk: "poison", t: { slug: "transaction", step: 5 }, p: { file: "src/main/java/com/example/bookstore/service/OrderService.java", from: 132, to: 139, note: "triggerInnerRollbackOnly swallows the exception, which is exactly why the failure looks recovered." } },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Transactions", n: "commit", q: "recordOrderPlaced is annotated @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.MANDATORY). What failure mode is that annotation chosen to prevent?", opts: ["The outbox event being written in its own transaction, so it could commit when the order rolls back","The event being written twice","The order being written before the stock reservation","The event being written without any transaction at all"], a: 0, e: "MANDATORY fails loudly instead of quietly starting a transaction of its own. If the caller ever loses its @Transactional, REQUIRED would silently commit the event on its own and the dual-write gap would reopen with no error at all. The captured run shows 2 of 2 events published and both delivered only after the order committed.", ev: "outbox", evk: "events", t: { slug: "transaction", step: 3 }, p: { file: "src/main/java/com/example/bookstore/service/OutboxService.java", from: 26, to: 27, note: "MANDATORY refuses to run without a caller transaction instead of creating one." } },
    { d: "Spring_Boot_Transactions", n: "outertx", q: "Switching the inner reservation to REQUIRES_NEW changes what the advised-method list reports. What does that mode actually change about atomicity?", opts: ["The reservation commits in its own suspended transaction, so it is no longer atomic with the order that depends on it","It makes the order commit earlier","It disables the outer transaction entirely","It rolls the outer transaction back if the inner one succeeds"], a: 0, e: "The captured report shows the different entry point (reserveCopyInNewTransaction) precisely so you can see advice applied to a second, independent transaction. The trade-off is real: the inner commit can succeed while the outer order later fails, which is exactly the dual-write problem the transactional outbox exists to avoid on the write side.", ev: "transactions", evk: "requires-new", t: { slug: "transaction", step: 4 }, p: { file: "src/main/java/com/example/bookstore/service/StockService.java", from: 27, to: 30, note: "REQUIRES_NEW always runs in its own transaction, suspended or not." } },

  ],
  troubleshooting: [
    { s: "java.net.BindException: Web server failed to start. Port 8080 was already in use.", cause: "Another process on the host holds the socket - typically a previous run of the same app, a leftover java.exe, or a container publishing the same host port.", fix: "Find the holder with netstat -ano | findstr :8080 and stop that PID, or change the port with server.port. In tests use server.port=0 and inject the actual port so parallel builds never collide.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Startup", "Spring_Boot_Deployment"], tags: ["startup", "ports"] },
    { s: "Description: No qualifying bean of type 'com.acme.OrderService' available: expected at least 1 bean.", cause: "No bean of that type is registered - the class is not a stereotype (@Service or @Component), or it sits outside the package tree scanned by your @SpringBootApplication.", fix: "Move the class into or below the application class's package and annotate it with @Service; if it is an interface, add an implementation bean. Set logging.level.org.springframework.beans.factory.support=TRACE to see the packages actually scanned.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Startup"], tags: ["startup", "wiring", "component-scan"] },
    { s: "NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException: expected single matching bean but found 2: smtpMailSender, sesMailSender", cause: "Two beans of the same type exist and the injection point does not say which one it wants.", fix: "Mark the preferred bean @Primary, or qualify the injection point with @Qualifier('smtpMailSender'). If the choice is per environment, use @Profile on each implementation instead of a qualifier.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Startup", "Spring_Boot_AntiPatterns"], tags: ["startup", "wiring"] },
    { s: "Parameter 0 of constructor in com.acme.PaymentService required a bean of type 'com.acme.PaymentClient' that could not be found", cause: "The constructor dependency has no implementation bean: a missing @Service stereotype, a package outside the component-scan root, or a @Bean method disabled by a condition or an inactive profile.", fix: "Check in order: the stereotype on the implementation, the package relative to your @SpringBootApplication class, whether the bean method's @ConditionalOn.../@Profile matches the active environment, and finally whether the client library is a declared dependency.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Startup", "Spring_Boot_AutoConfiguration"], tags: ["startup", "wiring", "conditional"] },
    { s: "BeanCurrentlyInCreationException: Error creating bean with name 'a': Requested bean is currently in creation: Is there an unresolvable circular reference?", cause: "Two or more beans depend on each other through constructors, so neither can be constructed first.", fix: "Break the cycle by extracting the shared behaviour into a third bean. If one dependency is genuinely optional, mark it @Lazy so the injected proxy defers construction, or use setter injection for the side that can tolerate it.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Startup", "Spring_Boot_Bean_Lifecycle"], tags: ["startup", "wiring", "circular-dependency"] },
    { s: "Failed to configure a DataSource: 'url' attribute is not specified and no embedded datasource could be configured.", cause: "DataSourceAutoConfiguration is a candidate but no JDBC starter is on the classpath, or spring.datasource.url is unset - for example because it only exists in an application-dev.properties file while another profile is active.", fix: "Add spring-boot-starter-jdbc (or the JPA starter) and set spring.datasource.url; verify the active profile. Remember that environment variables outrank properties files, so a stale SPRING_DATASOURCE_URL will change the failure you see.", d: ["Spring_Boot_AutoConfiguration", "Spring_Boot_Config_Properties"], tags: ["startup", "database", "configuration"] },
    { s: "org.springframework.jdbc.CannotGetJdbcConnectionException: Could not create connection to database server / Caused by: java.lang.ClassNotFoundException: org.h2.Driver", cause: "The JDBC driver is not on the runtime classpath - a common result of H2 being a provided-scope or test-only dependency, or of a test that assumes H2 while production runs PostgreSQL or MySQL.", fix: "Add the driver with runtime (or compile) scope, or point tests at the engine you actually run in production using Testcontainers. H2's compatibility mode is not the same database, so an H2-only test suite proves very little about your real SQL and DDL.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Testing_Slices", "Spring_Boot_Migration_3"], tags: ["database", "testing", "classpath"] },
    { s: "org.postgresql.util.PSQLException: FATAL: too many connections for role 'app'", cause: "Total connections exceed the server limit. Typically every replica opens its own Hikari pool (default maximum-pool-size 10) and the count is multiplied by replicas, plus dev tools and monitoring - or a leak where connections are never returned.", fix: "Size the pool per replica so replicas x pool size stays well under max_connections with headroom for admin and migration sessions, set minimumIdle below maximumPoolSize, and enable Hikari's leakDetectionThreshold to find code that holds a connection outside a transaction.", d: ["Spring_Boot_AutoConfiguration", "Spring_Boot_Transactions"], tags: ["database", "performance", "pooling"] },
    { s: "HikariPool-1 - Connection is not available, request timed out after 30000ms. Total connections: 10, active: 10, idle: 0", cause: "Every pooled connection is checked out and never returned. Usual causes are long transactions with slow I/O inside them, REQUIRES_NEW nesting that needs a second connection, or a leak of raw connections or EntityManagers.", fix: "Move remote calls and file I/O outside the transaction, avoid REQUIRES_NEW in hot paths, and enable leakDetectionThreshold (above your slowest legitimate checkout) to get the leaking stack trace named. Raising maximum-pool-size only postpones the failure and can overload the database instead.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Transactions", "Spring_Boot_AntiPatterns"], tags: ["database", "performance", "pooling", "transactions"] },
    { s: "org.hibernate.LazyInitializationException: could not initialize proxy - no Session and current thread is not associated with this Hibernate Session", cause: "A lazy association is dereferenced after the transaction and session have closed - typically while Jackson serialises an entity returned from a service method.", fix: "Return a DTO built inside the transaction, or fetch what you need with @EntityGraph, a join fetch or a projection. Keep spring.jpa.open-in-view=false in production; enabling it hides the symptom while holding a database connection for the entire request.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Nplus1", "Spring_Boot_Transactions", "Spring_Boot_Layers"], tags: ["jpa", "lazy-loading", "osiv"] },
    { s: "WARNING: spring.jpa.open-in-view is enabled by default. Therefore, database queries may be performed during view rendering.", cause: "Open Session in View is on by default, so the EntityManager stays open for the whole HTTP request in order to allow lazy loading during serialisation.", fix: "Set spring.jpa.open-in-view=false and remove the lazy loading with DTOs, fetch joins or projections. Leaving it enabled means one pooled connection per in-flight request for the entire rendering, and queries appear in logs from code that never touched a repository.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Nplus1", "Spring_Boot_Transactions"], tags: ["jpa", "performance", "osiv"] },
    { s: "org.hibernate.exception.SQLGrammarException: could not extract ResultSet; nested cause PSQLException: ERROR: relation 'book' does not exist", cause: "The physical table name does not match the mapping - the naming strategy produced a different name, migrations never ran against this schema, or the entity is mapped to another schema.", fix: "Print the expected schema (temporarily set spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=create with SQL logging, or inspect a Flyway-generated migration) to learn the exact name, then align @Table or the naming strategy and ensure the migration creates it. Quote reserved words such as order explicitly.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Data_Model", "Spring_Boot_Migration_3"], tags: ["jpa", "sql", "schema"] },
    { s: "org.springframework.orm.jpa.JpaSystemException: could not execute statement [duplicate key value violates unique constraint 'uk_order_reference']", cause: "A uniqueness rule in the database is violated - a retried non-idempotent operation, two concurrent requests claiming the same reference, or the constraint was added after duplicates already existed.", fix: "Make the operation idempotent (insert-if-not-exists, or INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING) and let the database be the arbiter instead of a read in application code. Where the operation is safe to repeat, catch the unique violation and treat it as success, and backfill before adding the constraint.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Data_Model", "Spring_Boot_Transactions"], tags: ["database", "concurrency", "idempotency"] },
    { s: "org.springframework.transaction.UnexpectedRollbackException: Transaction silently rolled back because it has been marked as rollback-only", cause: "An inner @Transactional method (propagation REQUIRED) threw, the outer method caught the exception and carried on, but the shared transaction was already poisoned - or a rollback-only transaction escaped a non-transactional callback.", fix: "Either let the exception propagate and translate it to your API contract above the transaction boundary, or make the inner operation genuinely independent with REQUIRES_NEW (accepting the second connection). Also check for @Transactional methods called via this, which bypass the proxy and lose their annotation entirely.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Transactions", "Spring_Boot_AOP_Proxy"], tags: ["transactions", "error-handling"] },
    { s: "InvalidDataAccessApiUsageException: Executing an update/delete query", cause: "A modifying operation ran with no active transaction - the @Transactional was lost (an override without the annotation, a self-invocation, a final method, or a call from a non-transactional component) or the write happened in a read-only transaction.", fix: "Put @Transactional on the service method that performs the write, and verify it is public, on a non-final class, and invoked through the proxy rather than with a this call. For bulk operations use @Modifying with clearAutomatically and flushAutomatically so the persistence context does not serve stale entities.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Transactions", "Spring_Boot_AOP_Proxy"], tags: ["transactions", "jpa", "proxy"] },
    { s: "java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space (logs also show 'GC overhead limit exceeded')", cause: "Either a genuine leak - unbounded caches, static collections, streams never closed, or listeners that accumulate - or a single operation materialising far more rows than intended, or simply too small a heap for the working set.", fix: "Capture a heap dump at the moment of failure (-XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError or jcmd <pid> GC.heap_dump) and diff two dumps to find what grows. Fix the largest retainer - usually a cache without a maximum size or an accidental cartesian join - before raising the heap, and prefer MaxRAMPercentage in containers.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Caching", "Spring_Boot_Deployment"], tags: ["memory", "performance", "caching"] },
    { s: "Container status CrashLoopBackOff; the log ends with java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: unable to create new native thread, or the pod is killed with exit code 137 and no Java error at all", cause: "Either thread exhaustion - an unbounded @Async executor, a runaway scheduled loop, or a per-request pool created inside a singleton - or the cgroup memory limit was consumed by heap plus metaspace, direct buffers and thread stacks.", fix: "Define a ThreadPoolTaskExecutor with a fixed core and max size, a bounded queue and an explicit rejection policy, then size the JVM with -XX:MaxRAMPercentage leaving headroom for native memory, and cap the Hikari pool so replica scaling cannot multiply connections.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Async_Scheduling", "Spring_Boot_Deployment"], tags: ["memory", "threads", "containers", "async"] },
    { s: "401 {\"error\":\"invalid_token\"} on requests that worked before a key rotation, and clock skew is suspected", cause: "The JWK set is cached by the resource server, so a rotated signing key is not picked up immediately; or issuer/nbf validation is failing because the clocks differ by more than the allowed skew.", fix: "Configure the resource server with spring.security.oauth2.resourceserver.jwt.jwk-set-uri so the Nimbus decoder refreshes keys, shorten the JWK cache TTL if rotation is frequent, and verify NTP synchronisation on both hosts. Also confirm the token was signed by the key the issuer currently publishes, not the retired one.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Security_JWT"], tags: ["security", "jwt", "auth"] },
    { s: "403 on a browser form POST, while the same call with curl and a Bearer token succeeds", cause: "CSRF protection is enabled by default for browser clients. A form POST carries the session cookie automatically, so the request is rejected for a missing or invalid CSRF token; a request authenticated only by the Authorization header is not subject to it.", fix: "Keep CSRF enabled for cookie or session based clients and send the token as the _csrf form field, the X-CSRF-TOKEN header, or via CookieCsrfTokenRepository.withHttpOnlyFalse. Disable CSRF only for a genuinely stateless bearer-token API, where no ambient credential can be ridden on.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Security_JWT"], tags: ["security", "csrf", "http"] },
    { s: "HTTP 415 Unsupported Media Type on a POST that works from Postman but not from the browser", cause: "The Content-Type of the request body does not match what the handler can consume - typically a form-encoded body sent to a @RequestBody endpoint, or a missing content type on a fetch call.", fix: "Send application/json and make sure the JSON is what the DTO expects; if you deliberately support both, declare consumes = {MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON_VALUE, MediaType.APPLICATION_FORM_URLENCODED_VALUE} or add a second handler. Check the response body for the list of supported media types, which the error usually includes.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Layers", "Spring_Boot_Request_Path"], tags: ["http", "json", "content-negotiation"] },
    { s: "InvalidDefinitionException: Java 8 date/time type java.time.LocalDate not supported by default: add Module 'com.fasterxml.jackson.datatype:jackson-datatype-jsr310' to get supported", cause: "The JSR-310 module is not registered, so Jackson cannot map java.time types. It is usually present transitively but excluded, or auto-configuration was disabled.", fix: "Check the spring-boot-starter-json and jackson-datatype-jsr310 dependencies and exclude whatever is shadowing them, and make sure Jackson auto-configuration is not switched off. The same root cause produces silently string-formatted dates once it is working, so configure the date format explicitly rather than relying on defaults.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Testing_Slices", "Spring_Boot_Layers"], tags: ["json", "classpath", "serialization"] },
    { s: "A single query takes 30 seconds and EXPLAIN shows a sequential scan on a table with a few million rows", cause: "A missing or unusable index - the column in the predicate or join is not indexed, or the index exists but the query shape prevents its use (a function applied to the column, a leading wildcard, or a type mismatch).", fix: "Read the execution plan, add the index that matches the actual predicate and join, and remove anything that makes the column non-sargable such as to_char(), DATE(col) = or LIKE '%x'. Keep the pool sized for the concurrency the index enables, because a faster query changes the load profile rather than the bottleneck.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Nplus1", "Spring_Boot_Data_Model"], tags: ["performance", "sql", "database"] },
    { s: "401 on every endpoint immediately after adding spring-boot-starter-security to an existing internal API", cause: "The security starter auto-configures a default filter chain that requires authentication for every URL and logs a generated password; no application SecurityFilterChain bean was declared to replace it.", fix: "Declare your own SecurityFilterChain bean permitting the genuinely public paths (actuator health, login, docs) and authenticating the rest, or exclude the starter entirely for an internal service on a trusted network. Remember the auto-configured chain applies to every path until you define your own bean.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Security_JWT", "Spring_Boot_AutoConfiguration"], tags: ["security", "http", "auto-configuration"] },
    { s: "404 Not Found for a path that clearly has a matching @GetMapping", cause: "The request never reached a handler: the URI does not match (trailing slash, case, or a PathPattern versus suffix-pattern difference), a context path or proxy prefix was added, or a request matcher returned 404 for an unauthenticated request instead of 401.", fix: "Set logging.level.org.springframework.web.servlet.mvc.method.annotation.RequestMappingHandlerMapping=TRACE to list the mappings actually registered and compare with the exact request URI. Then check server.servlet.context-path, prefix handling in the reverse proxy, and whether request matchers run before permitAll.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Request_Path", "Spring_Boot_Security_JWT"], tags: ["http", "routing", "debugging"] },
    { s: "RequestMappingHandlerMapping: Ambiguous handler methods mapped for '/orders/{id}'", cause: "Two or more mappings match the same request - typically overlapping patterns such as /orders/{id} versus /orders/new, or a duplicate mapping inherited from an abstract base class.", fix: "Make the specific mapping unambiguous: constrain the variable with a regex ({id:[0-9]+}), move the static segment to a distinct path, or remove the overlapping inherited mapping. The same TRACE logging prints the competing patterns so you can see the collision directly.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Request_Path"], tags: ["http", "routing", "startup"] },
    { s: "HTTP 400 with HttpMessageNotReadableException: JSON parse error: Unexpected character ('}' ...) for a body that looks correct", cause: "The body is not valid JSON for the target type - a trailing comma, a double-encoded payload, an empty body on a required @RequestBody parameter, or a client sending form-encoded data to a JSON endpoint.", fix: "Log the raw body once (mindfully, it may contain secrets) and fix the client; make genuinely optional bodies @RequestBody(required = false) or Optional; and add an @ExceptionHandler(HttpMessageNotReadableException.class) that returns a clean problem response instead of a stack trace.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Layers"], tags: ["http", "json", "validation"] },
    { s: "MethodArgumentNotValidException on a POST where the JSON looks valid, or a jakarta.validation constraint that never fires", cause: "Bean validation failed on a nested object that was not annotated with @Valid, so its constraints were never evaluated; or validation groups were configured inconsistently between the DTO and the controller.", fix: "Read the BindingResult field errors, which name the exact field, and annotate nested DTOs with @Valid so the cascade works. Remember @Valid validates at the web boundary while @Validated on a class is what enables method-level constraints on a service, and both are needed only for their own layer.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Layers", "Spring_Boot_Testing_Slices"], tags: ["validation", "http"] },
    { s: "java.lang.ClassNotFoundException: javax.servlet.http.HttpServlet, or NoClassDefFoundError: javax/xml/bind/DatatypeConverter on Java 17", cause: "A pre-Jakarta dependency still references the javax APIs, and the servlet and JAXB modules that were bundled with the JDK before Java 9 are no longer present - the classic symptom of running a Boot 2 library inside a Boot 3 application.", fix: "Migrate the dependency to its Jakarta-based release rather than adding JAXB back to the classpath, and use jdeps or the compiler errors to find every remaining javax reference. The alternative is pinning that service to Java 11 and Boot 2, which is a legitimate architectural decision but should be explicit.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Migration_3"], tags: ["migration", "classpath", "java"] },
    { s: "The application starts but is not reachable from outside the container or from a Kubernetes service", cause: "The server bound to a specific interface, the container port is not published, the containerPort is not declared, or a context path is missing from the URL being called.", fix: "Check the startup line for the bound address and port, set server.address=0.0.0.0 if you have overridden it, publish or declare the port, and include the context path in the request. Test from inside the pod with curl before blaming the network.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Deployment", "Spring_Boot_Startup"], tags: ["deployment", "containers", "networking"] },
    { s: "Liveness probe fails with 503 and the pod restarts repeatedly, roughly when the database is slow", cause: "The liveness group is including the database (or another shared dependency) health indicator, so a dependency blip restarts every replica and turns a partial outage into a total one.", fix: "Separate the health groups: liveness includes only livenessState, readiness includes readinessState plus db or your composite. Confirm what is registered at /actuator/health/readiness and /actuator/health/liveness, and remember that the probes group only exists automatically on Kubernetes or when explicitly enabled.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Observability", "Spring_Boot_Deployment"], tags: ["observability", "deployment", "health"] },
    { s: "Readiness probe fails during a rolling deploy even though the new pods work once traffic arrives", cause: "The new pod is not yet serving - the context is still starting, or a migration is holding a lock - while the terminating pod has already been removed from the endpoints, so the service has no ready backends.", fix: "Add a startup probe for slow starts, keep readiness false until the application is genuinely ready, run migrations as a separate step rather than in application startup, and widen terminationGracePeriodSeconds with a preStop sleep so in-flight requests finish.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Deployment", "Spring_Boot_Observability"], tags: ["deployment", "observability", "reliability"] },
    { s: "Spring Boot 3 migration: an old property such as spring.redis.host is ignored and a connection is attempted against localhost", cause: "Property namespaces moved between major versions (for example spring.redis.* to spring.data.redis.*) and unmapped keys are silently ignored rather than rejected.", fix: "Diff the effective configuration between versions using the configprops endpoint or an environment dump, enable strict binding during the migration so unknown keys fail fast, and update the deployment manifests and secret keys at the same time as the code.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Migration_3", "Spring_Boot_Config_Properties"], tags: ["migration", "configuration", "release"] },
    { s: "org.springframework.boot.actuate.autoconfigure.security.servlet.ManagementWebSecurityAutoConfiguration conflicts with your own rules, or /actuator/health returns 401", cause: "Actuator has its own management context path and, when Spring Security is present, its own security rules - so an application rule that permits all paths may not cover the management endpoints, and vice versa.", fix: "Use management.endpoints.web.base-path deliberately, add explicit matchers for the actuator paths in your SecurityFilterChain (ordering matters), and prefer binding management server to a separate port (management.server.port) so probes and metrics can be exposed on a different network surface than the API.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Observability", "Spring_Boot_Security_JWT"], tags: ["observability", "security", "http"] },
    { s: "Tests pass locally but fail in CI, with data missing after one test method", cause: "@DataJpaTest rolls back between methods, but the test opened its own transaction (REQUIRES_NEW, or TestTransaction) so its writes were committed and never cleaned up, contaminating later tests; or the dev database was used instead of a fresh one.", fix: "Keep one transaction per test method and avoid REQUIRES_NEW, or wrap the whole test in a cleanup strategy. Pin the test datasource with @ServiceConnection Testcontainers or an in-memory database, and never point tests at a shared developer database.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Testing_Slices"], tags: ["testing", "transactions", "database"] },
    { s: "Could not find a valid Docker environment (Testcontainers) in the build", cause: "The Docker daemon socket is not available to the test process: a remote or rootless daemon without DOCKER_HOST, a CI runner without a docker service, or Docker Desktop not running.", fix: "Point the client at the daemon with DOCKER_HOST and DOCKER_TLS_VERIFY, add a docker service to the CI job, or fall back to a GitHub Actions service container. Keep a fast, embedded-database suite for local development so the whole suite is not blocked on Docker.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Testing_Slices", "Spring_Boot_Deployment"], tags: ["testing", "containers", "ci"] },
    { s: "Scheduled job fires on every replica and downstream data is duplicated, or the same job silently stops running", cause: "Each replica runs the same @Scheduled method, and there is no distributed lock; separately, a single-threaded scheduler means one long-running job blocks every other job indefinitely.", fix: "Guard the job with a distributed lock (ShedLock on a shared store) or move it to a single-worker deployment, give the scheduler a pool, and alert on job completion. Make the job itself idempotent, because a lock alone does not protect against a crash between doing the work and recording it.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Async_Scheduling", "Spring_Boot_Deployment"], tags: ["async", "deployment", "concurrency"] },
    { s: "Logs from an async task contain a different traceId, and SecurityContextHolder returns an anonymous user inside the task", cause: "Thread-locals are not inherited by pool threads, so MDC, the SecurityContext and the servlet request context are empty or stale on the worker.", fix: "Add a TaskDecorator (or Micrometer context propagation with a ContextSnapshot) that captures and replays the context and clears it in a finally block so pooled threads do not leak state into the next task, and pass required data explicitly as method arguments where possible.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Async_Scheduling", "Spring_Boot_Observability"], tags: ["async", "observability", "threading"] },
    { s: "GET /actuator/prometheus returns 404, or the endpoint is missing entirely", cause: "No metrics registry is on the classpath, or the endpoint is not exposed over the web - exposure defaults to health and info only, and a non-web application has no HTTP endpoint at all.", fix: "Add micrometer-registry-prometheus, add prometheus to management.endpoints.web.exposure.include, and confirm the endpoint path with the management base path. Check /actuator to list what is actually exposed before changing anything else.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Observability"], tags: ["observability", "metrics", "actuator"] },
    { s: "Slow requests and a wall of nearly identical SELECT statements in the log, one per row", cause: "An N+1 select problem: a list is fetched with one query and each row's lazy association triggers another, usually during iteration or during JSON serialization.", fix: "Fetch what the response needs - @EntityGraph, join fetch, or a projection into a DTO - or enable batch fetching with hibernate.default_batch_fetch_size. Turn on statement logging briefly to confirm the pattern, and remember that a second to-many fetch needs a Set or a second query.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Nplus1"], tags: ["performance", "jpa", "sql"] },
    { s: "Answers are correct on one instance and stale on another; a cache eviction on one replica does not affect the rest", cause: "A local cache manager (Caffeine or ConcurrentMapCacheManager) keeps entries per JVM, so replicas have independent caches and independent eviction timing.", fix: "Move to a shared cache manager such as Redis, or accept bounded staleness with a short TTL and make the UI tolerate it. Whichever you pick, record it, because 'works on instance 2' is not a bug report anyone can act on.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Caching"], tags: ["caching", "scaling", "consistency"] },
    { s: "@CacheEvict and @Async annotations appear to be ignored on some methods", cause: "Both are proxy-based advice, and those methods are called internally with this or invoked on a class you instantiated yourself, so no proxy is involved.", fix: "Move the annotated methods to another bean, or self-inject the proxy (ObjectProvider or a lazy self reference) and call through it. Audit for new this-calls when a new cross-cutting annotation is added - it is the most common cause of silently inert advice.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Caching", "Spring_Boot_Async_Scheduling", "Spring_Boot_AOP_Proxy"], tags: ["aop", "caching", "proxy"] },
    { s: "With spring-boot-starter-web and spring-boot-starter-webflux both present, requests behave as blocking MVC and throughput is flat", cause: "WebApplicationType deduction prefers SERVLET when the servlet classes are on the classpath, so the reactive stack is silently unused - typically because a transitive dependency pulled in spring-boot-starter-web.", fix: "Find the culprit with a dependency tree and exclude the MVC starter, or set spring.main.web-application-type=reactive to make the choice explicit. Then verify the server startup line no longer reports Tomcat, and check that no blocking database call is on the event loop.", d: ["Spring_Boot_WebFlux"], tags: ["webflux", "classpath", "performance"] },
    { s: "Timestamps in the log are out of order and one request's lines are interleaved with another's", cause: "Concurrent request processing plus asynchronous and reactive execution, so a chronological sort of the log is not a valid way to reconstruct a request.", fix: "Put the correlation identifiers in the log - traceId and spanId from Micrometer, plus your own request id - and group by them in your log backend rather than by timestamp. This is also the fastest way to see the wall-clock cost of a request end to end.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Observability"], tags: ["observability", "logs", "debugging"] },
    { s: "A message-driven flow loses or duplicates side effects: the order is rolled back but the notification still went out, or the same notification arrives twice", cause: "The dual-write problem: the message was published inside a transaction, so it can be sent before the commit (the consumer sees no data) or survive a rollback; and at-least-once delivery redelivers on failure or rebalance.", fix: "Use the transactional outbox - order plus an intent row committed together, published by a separate relay - and make the consumer idempotent with a unique constraint on the message or business key written in the same transaction as the effect.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Messaging"], tags: ["messaging", "reliability", "transactions"] },
    { s: "The same @Scheduled job runs on every replica and duplicates work, or jobs run minutes late", cause: "Each replica executes the schedule independently, and the default scheduler is single-threaded so one slow job delays all the others.", fix: "Guard the job with a distributed lock or move it to a single-worker deployment, give the scheduler a pool, and make the job idempotent because a lock does not protect against a crash after the work. Monitor job duration and last-success time, not just that the job exists.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Async_Scheduling", "Spring_Boot_Deployment"], tags: ["async", "deployment", "concurrency"] },
    { s: "Deployments cause a burst of 502 or connection reset responses even though the pods are healthy", cause: "The termination signal arrives while requests are still in flight, and the pod leaves the endpoints and starts closing the connector at the same time.", fix: "Enable graceful shutdown with a bounded per-phase timeout, add a preStop sleep so endpoint removal propagates before the kill, and make terminationGracePeriodSeconds longer than your slowest request. Scale up before draining so there is always spare capacity.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Deployment"], tags: ["deployment", "reliability", "http"] },
    { s: "The application ignores your configuration file entirely after a refactor, or picks up a different value than expected", cause: "Configuration precedence: command line arguments and environment variables outrank profile-specific and packaged property files, and property namespaces moved between major Boot versions so old keys are silently ignored.", fix: "Print the effective configuration (the configprops endpoint, or an environment dump at startup) and compare it with the file, check the active profile, and remove stale environment variables from the deployment manifests. In tests, @TestPropertySource and inlined properties outrank everything else, which is its own surprise.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Config_Properties", "Spring_Boot_Migration_3"], tags: ["configuration", "deployment", "debugging"] },
  ],
  interview: [
    { q: "What is Spring Boot, and what does it actually give you over plain Spring Framework?", a: "Spring Boot is a set of auto-configuration, starter dependencies and embedded runtime conventions on top of Spring Framework. Mechanism: it detects classes on the classpath, imports matching auto-configurations, binds configuration to typed properties, and starts an embedded server. Tradeoff: magic means surprises - a default you did not know about can change behaviour, so you read the auto-configuration report. When it bites: when a bean is missing or a default binds unexpectedly, and the fix is usually the condition report rather than a guess.", level: "junior", d: ["Spring_Boot_AutoConfiguration", "Spring_Boot_Roadmap"] },
    { q: "Explain dependency injection and constructor injection. Why is one form preferred?", a: "DI means the container supplies a collaborator rather than the class constructing it, which decouples the service from its dependency and makes the dependency explicit. Constructor injection makes the dependency part of the type's signature, allows final fields, and fails fast at startup when it cannot be satisfied. Field injection hides dependencies and requires a container or reflection to test. It bites whenever someone refactors a class and silently adds a field injection nobody reviewed.", level: "junior", d: ["Spring_Boot_Startup", "Spring_Boot_Layers"] },
    { q: "What is the ApplicationContext, and how does it differ from a plain BeanFactory?", a: "The ApplicationContext is the central interface that extends BeanFactory with enterprise features: internationalisation, event publishing, resource loading, and automatic detection of annotated components. Mechanism: it holds bean definitions, instantiates singletons through BeanPostProcessors, and publishes lifecycle events. Tradeoff: the extra features make it a service locator if you inject it everywhere. It bites when a class holds the context to look beans up dynamically, which is the service-locator anti-pattern in disguise.", level: "junior", d: ["Spring_Boot_Startup", "Spring_Boot_Bean_Lifecycle"] },
    { q: "What happens between pressing run and the first accepted HTTP request in a Spring Boot application?", a: "The main method calls SpringApplication.run, which prepares the environment, creates the context, applies auto-configuration, refreshes the context - instantiating and wiring singletons - and starts the embedded web server, finishing with ApplicationReadyEvent. Any failure during refresh aborts startup with a BeanCreationException. The trade-off is that most errors appear at startup rather than at first use, which is good, but a failure in a BeanPostProcessor or a condition can produce a very indirect message. It bites when a database migration is run during startup and every replica races for DDL locks.", level: "junior", d: ["Spring_Boot_Startup"] },
    { q: "How does a request get from the browser to your database and back? Name each hop.", a: "The browser sends the request to the connector, which builds the servlet filter chain, so authentication, logging and CORS filters run first. The DispatcherServlet then finds a handler with a HandlerMapping, invokes it through a HandlerAdapter with argument resolvers, and the controller delegates to a service, which goes through the transaction proxy to a repository and the database. The response travels back up the same chain, with serialization by Jackson. It bites when you put logic in the wrong layer: a lazy field touched during serialization, after the transaction has closed, is the classic LazyInitializationException.", level: "junior", d: ["Spring_Boot_Request_Path", "Spring_Boot_Layers"] },
    { q: "What is auto-configuration, and how would you override one bean without excluding the whole auto-configuration?", a: "Auto-configuration is a conditional bean definition supplied by a starter: it activates when the relevant classes are present, and backs off when you define the bean yourself. Mechanism: candidates come from AutoConfiguration.imports, and @ConditionalOnMissingBean is the back-off signal. So defining a bean of the same type in your configuration is enough - the auto-config steps aside. Tradeoff: because conditions are evaluated at parse time, ordering between auto-configurations is expressed with @AutoConfigureBefore/After, and blindly using @ConditionalOnBean on your own beans is fragile. It bites when a starter is missing entirely, so no candidate ever runs.", level: "mid", d: ["Spring_Boot_AutoConfiguration"] },
    { q: "How does @ConfigurationProperties binding work, and why is it better than @Value?", a: "The Environment is queried using relaxed binding rules: canonical kebab-case names map to env vars, system properties, YAML and profile-specific files, and the result is bound to a typed object with conversion, default values and JSR-380 validation. @Value is a single placeholder with no validation, no defaults beyond the placeholder, and poor error messages. Tradeoff: binding validates at startup, which means a missing variable fails deployment instead of production - usually what you want. It bites on naming, where APP_REGION resolves to app.region but DATASOURCE_URL does not resolve to spring.datasource.url.", level: "mid", d: ["Spring_Boot_Config_Properties"] },
    { q: "What is a Spring bean lifecycle callback, and in what order do the callbacks run?", a: "The container instantiates the bean, populates its properties through dependency injection, invokes Aware callbacks, then runs @PostConstruct, InitializingBean.afterPropertiesSet and a custom init-method in that order; on shutdown it runs @PreDestroy, DisposableBean.destroy and the custom destroy-method. Mechanism: BeanPostProcessors drive most of these, which is why a proxy is already in place when @PostConstruct runs. Tradeoff: initialisation order is a frequent source of surprise for resources acquired in init methods. It bites when @PostConstruct touches a lazy proxy that is not yet usable, or when init code calls a method that other beans have not finished building.", level: "mid", d: ["Spring_Boot_Bean_Lifecycle"] },
    { q: "Explain @Transactional: what does the annotation actually cause, and where does the boundary sit?", a: "The annotation is advice on a proxy; when a call arrives through the proxy, the transaction manager begins a transaction on the current thread, binds a connection to it, runs the method, then commits or rolls back and unbinds. The boundary is the annotated public method on a proxied bean. Tradeoff: the database gives you isolation, and you choose the scope - too wide wastes locks and connections, too narrow loses atomicity. It bites in three classic ways: self-invocation bypasses the proxy, private or final methods cannot be advised, and checked exceptions do not roll back by default.", level: "mid", d: ["Spring_Boot_Transactions", "Spring_Boot_AOP_Proxy"] },
    { q: "What are transaction propagation levels, and when would you use each one?", a: "REQUIRED (the default) joins the current transaction or creates one; REQUIRES_NEW suspends it and starts a genuinely independent transaction on a second connection; NESTED uses a savepoint inside the current transaction; NOT_SUPPORTED and READ_ONLY suspend or relax the current transaction. Mechanism: the transaction manager looks at the current transaction state through TransactionSynchronizationManager and binds a new one. Use REQUIRES_NEW for records that must survive the outer failure - audit rows, error logs - and NESTED for partial rollback within one unit of work. It bites with pool exhaustion, because a suspended transaction still holds its connection while the new one checks out another.", level: "mid", d: ["Spring_Boot_Transactions"] },
    { q: "Why does UnexpectedRollbackException appear even though the exception was caught, and what are your options?", a: "The inner call joined the outer transaction, so the failure marked the shared transaction rollback-only; catching the exception in application code does not clear that flag, and the commit attempt at the end fails loudly. Options: let the exception propagate, rethrow a translated exception above the boundary, or isolate the inner work with REQUIRES_NEW if it must survive. It bites hardest in flows that try to be resilient - partial success handling - where the developer reasonably believes the failure was handled.", level: "mid", d: ["Spring_Boot_Transactions"] },
    { q: "What is the N+1 select problem, and what are the remedies in order of preference?", a: "It is one query to fetch a list plus one query per row to initialise a lazy association, so the query count scales with the result set. Remedies: fetch what you need with a join fetch or @EntityGraph, project straight into a DTO, enable batch fetching (hibernate.default_batch_fetch_size), or map one collection as a Set. Preference order matters: a projection is often better than a join fetch, because it avoids loading entities you never use. It bites hardest with pagination, where in-memory pagination over a fetch-joined collection silently returns wrong page sizes.", level: "mid", d: ["Spring_Boot_Nplus1"] },
    { q: "How does Spring's @Cacheable work, and what are the invalidation strategies?", a: "Cache abstraction sits between your method and its logic: a CacheInterceptor looks up a Cache from the CacheManager, returns the stored value on a hit, and on a miss invokes the method and stores the result. That is cache-aside. Invalidation is evict-on-write with @CacheEvict, update-in-place with @CachePut, or time-based expiry; for lists, allEntries eviction or versioned keys. Tradeoff: caches hide staleness and make debugging harder, so a short TTL plus explicit eviction is safer than clever invalidation. It bites with multi-instance deployments, where a local cache means each replica has its own eviction timing.", level: "mid", d: ["Spring_Boot_Caching"] },
    { q: "How does Spring Security handle a request, and what is the role of the filter chain?", a: "Spring Security is built on the servlet filter chain: SecurityFilterChain implementations form a FilterChainProxy that runs filters in a defined order, with authentication filters populating a SecurityContext and authorization filters deciding access, followed by exception translation that distinguishes unauthenticated (entry point) from unauthorized (denied). Mechanism: the SecurityContextHolder is a thread-local, which is why it is cleared per request. Tradeoff: the whole security model is expressed as filters and matchers, which is powerful and easy to mis-order. It bites when a rule is declared on a path that is never actually requested, or when an exception is thrown before the exception translation filter runs.", level: "mid", d: ["Spring_Boot_Security_JWT"] },
    { q: "How does a stateless JWT API authenticate a request, and what security decisions are involved?", a: "The resource server filter parses the bearer token, validates its signature against keys fetched from the issuer's JWKS, checks issuer, audience and expiry, and maps claims into authorities; the result becomes an Authentication in the SecurityContext for that request only. Decisions: which signing keys are trusted, where roles come from, and whether the API is behind TLS. It bites with clock skew, key rotation and with a permissive default that treats any successful signature as full access.", level: "mid", d: ["Spring_Boot_Security_JWT"] },
    { q: "Why is field injection with @Autowired considered an anti-pattern?", a: "Because it makes dependencies invisible, allows objects to exist with null fields, and forces tests to use a container or reflection instead of a constructor. Constructor injection makes the dependency list part of the type, permits final fields, and gives a compile-time guarantee. Tradeoff: constructor injection can make classes with many dependencies look awkward, which is a design signal rather than an argument for the alternative. It bites in refactorings, when dependencies are added without changing any signature and nobody reviews the diff.", level: "junior", d: ["Spring_Boot_AntiPatterns"] },
    { q: "What does @Async do, and why is the proxy requirement important?", a: "@Async is advice that hands the invocation to a TaskExecutor and returns immediately, optionally with a CompletableFuture. Because it is proxy-based, the call must arrive through the proxy: a this call or a private method is never intercepted and runs synchronously, which is the usual reason an async method seems not to work. Tradeoff: you lose the thread's context, so MDC, the SecurityContext and the transaction are absent on the worker. It bites with an unbounded default executor, which turns a traffic spike into an out-of-memory error.", level: "mid", d: ["Spring_Boot_Async_Scheduling"] },
    { q: "How do you make logs, traces and security context follow an async task?", a: "Thread-locals do not propagate, so you must copy them explicitly: a TaskDecorator for @Async and scheduled tasks, Micrometer's context-propagation library with ContextSnapshot for arbitrary reactive and imperative boundaries, and a TaskDecorator for schedulers so MDC, SecurityContext and RequestContextHolder are captured and always cleared. Tradeoff: propagation must be bounded, otherwise a long-lived request context is retained on a pool thread. It bites most visibly in observability, when every async log line looks like a separate incident.", level: "senior", d: ["Spring_Boot_Async_Scheduling", "Spring_Boot_Observability"] },
    { q: "How would you design a system that publishes an event reliably after committing an order?", a: "Use the transactional outbox: write the order and an outbox row in one database transaction, then have a separate relay publish to the broker and mark rows as sent, with retries and a dead-letter path. The honest delivery guarantee is at-least-once, so consumers dedupe using a unique constraint written in the same transaction as the effect. Tradeoff: added latency from polling and operational complexity, in exchange for never losing a message. It bites in the ordering and duplicate handling, not in the publishing.", level: "senior", d: ["Spring_Boot_Messaging"] },
    { q: "What are the guarantees of a message broker, and how do you make a consumer idempotent?", a: "Most brokers give at-least-once delivery per consumer: messages can be redelivered after a rebalance, a restart or a processing failure, and ordering is only guaranteed within a partition. Idempotency comes from the consumer side - a unique constraint on a message id or business key, written atomically with the effect, or an inbox table - so a duplicate insert fails harmlessly. Tradeoff: exactly-once processing needs deduplication plus idempotent effects, not just a broker setting. It bites when the deduplication record and the effect are in different transactions, which reintroduces the duplicate.", level: "senior", d: ["Spring_Boot_Messaging"] },
    { q: "What are Micrometer, the actuator and OpenTelemetry, and how do they relate?", a: "Actuator exposes operational endpoints, Micrometer is the metrics facade that records meters, timers and distributions and publishes them to a registry, and OpenTelemetry supplies distributed tracing and context propagation. In Spring Boot, Micrometer Observation is the instrumenting API, and Micrometer Tracing bridges it to a tracer such as the OTel SDK, which then exports over OTLP. Tradeoff: each layer is useful alone, and adding all three means learning three vocabularies. It bites with cardinality explosion in metrics and with sampling that hides the errors you need.", level: "senior", d: ["Spring_Boot_Observability"] },
    { q: "How do liveness and readiness probes differ, and why does mixing them cause outages?", a: "Liveness asks whether the process should be restarted, so it must only check things a restart fixes; readiness asks whether the process should receive traffic, so it may include database connectivity, cache warm-up and downstream dependencies. Mixing them means a database blip restarts every replica simultaneously and turns a dependency outage into a full outage. In Boot you express this with health groups under the probes group. Tradeoff: stricter readiness means fewer bad requests but also fewer healthy pods during partial degradation.", level: "senior", d: ["Spring_Boot_Observability", "Spring_Boot_Deployment"] },
    { q: "How do you size a connection pool for a containerised service?", a: "Work from the database's capacity: total connections across all replicas must fit under the server's limit with headroom for admin, migrations and monitoring, so pool size per replica is a division, not a guess. Then keep transactions short so connections are returned quickly, and use a bounded pool for @Async work separately. Tradeoff: a larger pool improves per-instance throughput but can exhaust the database and increase contention. It bites under autoscaling, when a spike in replicas multiplies the connection count at exactly the moment the database is already struggling.", level: "senior", d: ["Spring_Boot_AutoConfiguration", "Spring_Boot_Deployment"] },
    { q: "Platform threads versus virtual threads: when does each make sense in a Spring Boot service?", a: "Platform threads are expensive to create and hold an OS stack, so throughput is bounded by the pool size; virtual threads (Java 21, enabled with spring.threads.virtual.enabled) are cheap and let blocking code scale, provided the real resource - the database pool, a downstream rate limit - is not the constraint. With virtual threads you still need pool-like limits on the scarce resource, and pinning or synchronized blocks can negate the benefit. Tradeoff: virtual threads simplify blocking-heavy services, but reactive stacks are still the better fit for very high concurrency per instance.", level: "senior", d: ["Spring_Boot_Async_Scheduling", "Spring_Boot_WebFlux"] },
    { q: "What is graceful shutdown, and what does a zero-downtime deployment need besides it?", a: "Graceful shutdown stops accepting new requests, lets in-flight requests finish within a timeout, and closes the context and the pool. For a rolling update you also need readiness to turn false before termination, a preStop delay so endpoint removal propagates, and a termination grace period longer than your slowest request. Tradeoff: a longer grace period makes rollouts slower and leaves a pod draining for that time. It bites when in-flight requests are longer than the grace period, which silently becomes a truncated response.", level: "mid", d: ["Spring_Boot_Deployment"] },
    { q: "Why do you deploy a database migration as a separate step rather than from application startup?", a: "Schema changes and application rollout have different failure modes and different rollback semantics: once data has been transformed, rolling the code back may be impossible, so the schema must be backward compatible first, then the code, then the cleanup. Running migrations at startup couples them to pod lifecycle, makes concurrent replicas race for DDL locks, and turns a slow migration into a failing rollout. Tradeoff: a separate migration step is an extra pipeline stage and needs its own permissions and audit. It bites when the change is not backward compatible and the old version is still serving traffic during a rolling deploy.", level: "senior", d: ["Spring_Boot_Deployment", "Spring_Boot_Migration_3"] },
    { q: "What are the main layers in a Spring Boot web application, and what belongs in each?", a: "Browser, then servlet filters (security, logging, CORS), the DispatcherServlet, controller, DTO, service, and then the persistence stack - repository, JPA/Hibernate, database - with cross-cutting concerns such as transactions and AOP around the service. Controllers speak HTTP and validate; services own use cases and transaction boundaries; repositories speak persistence. The layering matters because it lets the persistence model change without breaking the API, and it keeps transaction scope in one place. It bites when a controller returns entities directly, which couples the database schema to your public contract and moves lazy loading into serialisation.", level: "junior", d: ["Spring_Boot_Layers"] },
    { q: "How would you design a REST API's error handling in Spring Boot?", a: "A @RestControllerAdvice with specific @ExceptionHandler methods per exception family, mapping each to a status and a consistent problem body (type, title, status, detail, plus a trace identifier), with validation errors reported field by field. Reserve the catch-all for genuinely unexpected failures, log it with the cause, and return a generic body. Tradeoff: a rich error contract is a real API surface that clients depend on, so it is worth versioning deliberately. It bites when a catch-all flattens everything to 500, which silently breaks clients that branch on 400 or 404.", level: "mid", d: ["Spring_Boot_Layers", "Spring_Boot_AntiPatterns"] },
    { q: "What are DTOs and why do you map between entities and DTOs?", a: "A DTO is a data shape defined for a use case, decoupled from both the database schema and the internal model. Mapping gives you control over which fields are exposed, where validation lives, and how you version an API without a migration. Tradeoff: mapping code is boilerplate, and hand-written mappers drift; that is why records, MapStruct and interface-based mappers are popular. It bites with high-traffic paths where per-request allocation and reflection cost shows up, and with bidirectional entity graphs that would serialise infinitely if you returned them.", level: "junior", d: ["Spring_Boot_Layers", "Spring_Boot_Data_Model"] },
    { q: "What is the difference between @Valid and @Validated, and where does each belong?", a: "@Valid is the specification-driven annotation the web layer uses to trigger cascading validation of a request object; @Validated is Spring's annotation that adds method-level validation (constraint annotations directly on parameters) and groups support, so it belongs on a service class. Mechanism: both are processed by the same Bean Validation engine, but the trigger points differ. Tradeoff: knowing which one you needed saves a long debugging session. It bites when a service constraint is written without @Validated, so it is silently never evaluated.", level: "junior", d: ["Spring_Boot_Layers"] },
    { q: "How do you design a JPA entity model for a typical commerce domain?", a: "Entities own their columns and relationships; a parent owns the collection only as a mappedBy inverse view, while the child carries the foreign key and the owning side. Use an embedded composite key where the identity is genuinely composite, a business key or a surrogate id deliberately, and string enums so the database is readable. Tradeoff: bidirectional associations are convenient and produce infinite or N+1 problems on serialisation; unidirectional ones with explicit joins are boring and safe. It bites when the inverse side is treated as writable, or when equals/hashCode uses a generated id and objects vanish from sets after flush.", level: "mid", d: ["Spring_Boot_Data_Model"] },
    { q: "What is LAZY versus EAGER, and what are the JPA defaults?", a: "EAGER loads the association as part of the owning query; LAZY returns a proxy that loads on first access. The defaults are asymmetric: @ManyToOne is EAGER and @OneToMany is LAZY, which is why an entity with several eager associations produces a cartesian-join explosion. Tradeoff: lazy is not free - access after the session closes throws LazyInitializationException, and access inside a loop produces N+1. The right question is which shape each use case needs, expressed with fetch joins or entity graphs. It bites when someone flips one association to LAZY to fix a query and creates a worse problem somewhere else.", level: "mid", d: ["Spring_Boot_Data_Model", "Spring_Boot_Nplus1"] },
    { q: "What is the persistence context, and what does Open Session in View do?", a: "The EntityManager maintains a first-level cache within a transaction or request, so loading the same entity twice returns the same instance and dirty checking works on managed objects. Open Session in View keeps that session bound to the thread for the entire HTTP request so lazy loading still works during serialisation. Tradeoff: OSIV trades resource usage for convenience - one pooled connection per in-flight request for the whole rendering, and queries that appear from code that never touched a repository. The modern default is OSIV off plus DTOs. It bites hardest under load, where the connection pool exhausts for reasons that look unrelated to the database.", level: "senior", d: ["Spring_Boot_Nplus1", "Spring_Boot_Transactions"] },
    { q: "How do you fetch exactly the data a screen needs?", a: "Choose deliberately: a projection query or JPQL constructor expression straight into a DTO for read paths, a join fetch or @EntityGraph for entity graphs, and batch fetching as a fallback for several small loads. Tradeoff: projections are the most efficient and the least flexible - no lazy loading, no persistence semantics, and you write out every column. It bites with paging over a fetch-joined collection, where the join happens in memory and the page size is wrong.", level: "senior", d: ["Spring_Boot_Nplus1"] },
    { q: "How does Spring AOP work, and what are its limits?", a: "Spring AOP is proxy-based: the container wraps the bean in a JDK dynamic proxy (when interfaces are used) or a CGLIB subclass, and the advice runs when calls arrive through that proxy. Limits follow directly: no self-invocation, no private or final methods, and no interception of objects the container does not manage. Tradeoff: proxy-based advice is unobtrusive and requires no bytecode weaving, which is exactly why it is invisible until you hit one of those limits. It bites with @Transactional, @Async, @Cacheable and @Retryable, all of which fail silently rather than loudly.", level: "mid", d: ["Spring_Boot_AOP_Proxy"] },
    { q: "How do you measure and control database connection usage?", a: "Bound the pool per instance (Hikari by default), keep it small enough that replicas x pool size stays under the database limit, keep transactions short so connections are returned promptly, and use leakDetectionThreshold to catch connections held outside a transaction. Tradeoff: a bigger pool improves per-instance throughput but increases contention and can exhaust the server, so sizing is a function of the database's capacity, not of the framework. It bites under autoscaling, when the connection count multiplies exactly when the database is already loaded.", level: "senior", d: ["Spring_Boot_AutoConfiguration", "Spring_Boot_Transactions"] },
    { q: "What is the difference between JPA and R2DBC, and when would you use each?", a: "JPA is a blocking, specification-rich object-relational mapper with first- and second-level caching, dirty checking and a mature query language; R2DBC is a non-blocking Reactive Streams driver that leaves mapping, repositories and transactions to you. Choose JPA for the write-heavy, logic-heavy core where entity semantics pay off, and R2DBC where concurrency per instance dominates and everything in the path can be non-blocking. Tradeoff: mixing them means a reactive request thread blocking on JDBC, which is the worst of both. It bites when a service is reactive but one repository is JPA, and throughput stays flat at low CPU.", level: "senior", d: ["Spring_Boot_WebFlux"] },
    { q: "What problem does WebFlux solve, and when is it the wrong choice?", a: "WebFlux lets a thread handle many concurrent requests by never blocking - a few event loops drive non-blocking I/O, so throughput scales with the number of connections rather than the number of threads. It is the wrong choice for CPU-bound work, for services dominated by JDBC access, or when a large part of the stack has no non-blocking client. Tradeoff: it imposes reactive types and operator-composition expertise throughout the code. It bites the moment a blocking library is called on the event loop, and the symptom - high latency at low CPU - is easy to misdiagnose.", level: "senior", d: ["Spring_Boot_WebFlux"] },
    { q: "What are the test slices in Spring Boot, and how do you choose between them?", a: "There is a unit level with no Spring, a @JsonTest for serialisation, a @WebMvcTest for the web layer with mocked collaborators, a @DataJpaTest for repositories on an embedded database, a @RestClientTest for HTTP clients against a mock server, and a @SpringBootTest with Testcontainers for real integration. Choose the narrowest slice that can fail for the reason you care about, and pay for a full context only when the failure mode is wiring. Tradeoff: narrow slices are fast and precise but can mock away the very thing that breaks. It bites with @WebMvcTest - it does not load your services, so a broken service contract passes until integration tests.", level: "mid", d: ["Spring_Boot_Testing_Slices"] },
    { q: "What are mocks for, and what should you never mock?", a: "Mock at your own boundaries - network clients, clocks, message brokers, third-party SDKs - so tests are fast, deterministic and independent. Never mock the thing whose behaviour you are trying to verify: your repositories, the framework, or your own collaborators whose contract is in question. Tradeoff: a test full of mocks asserts your own assumptions instead of reality. It bites with over-verified interactions, where renaming a private method breaks a test that was checking the implementation rather than the outcome.", level: "mid", d: ["Spring_Boot_Testing_Slices", "Spring_Boot_AntiPatterns"] },
    { q: "What is Testcontainers for, and what does it change about your test strategy?", a: "It starts real dependencies - database, broker, cache - as Docker containers for the lifetime of the test, wiring them through @ServiceConnection so Boot uses them automatically. The payoff is fidelity: real dialect, real locking, real migrations, so a test cannot pass on H2 and fail in production. Tradeoff: containers need Docker and add seconds to startup, so they belong in a small integration suite while fast unit and slice tests run on embedded substitutes. It bites when the whole suite depends on Docker and local development slows to a crawl.", level: "mid", d: ["Spring_Boot_Testing_Slices"] },
    { q: "What is a backward-compatible schema change, and why does it matter for rolling deployments?", a: "A change the previous application version can keep running against - add a nullable column, add a table, stop writing a column before you start relying on it, backfill in batches, and only then enforce constraints. During a rolling update both versions serve traffic, so anything destructive breaks the old pods. Tradeoff: the expand-migrate-contract sequence takes longer and leaves temporary code. It bites when a rename or a NOT NULL addition is deployed in the same release as the code that depends on it, and the rollout is stuck halfway.", level: "senior", d: ["Spring_Boot_Migration_3", "Spring_Boot_Deployment"] },
    { q: "What does migrating from Spring Boot 2 to 3 actually involve?", a: "Move to Java 17 and Spring Framework 6, switch javax to jakarta across persistence, validation, servlet and annotations, rebuild dependencies against the new namespace, adapt Spring Security 6 to the lambda DSL, update moved property namespaces, and expect Hibernate 6 differences in naming and type handling. The compiler is your friend for the namespace work; the failure mode to fear is silent - an unmapped property or a changed default. Tradeoff: a staged migration with both versions running side by side is more work but far safer. It bites in third-party libraries that have not been rebuilt, and in tests relying on H2 or dialect behaviour that changed.", level: "senior", d: ["Spring_Boot_Migration_3"] },
    { q: "How do you make a Spring Boot application observable in production?", a: "Expose health, metrics and structured logs through the actuator, publish metrics via Micrometer to a Prometheus-compatible registry, instrument with Micrometer Observation so HTTP, JDBC and messaging produce spans, export traces over OTLP, and put trace and span identifiers in every log line. Tradeoff: instrumentation has a cost and cardinality has a limit, so name and label deliberately. It bites when a metric has a user id in a tag, which is the fastest way to take down a metrics backend.", level: "senior", d: ["Spring_Boot_Observability"] },
    { q: "Why can a long transaction hurt a database, and what is the guideline?", a: "A transaction holds locks and a pooled connection for its whole duration, so a long one increases lock waits, blocks concurrent work, delays vacuum or log flushing, and can exhaust the connection pool. Guideline: keep the transaction as short as possible, containing only the state change, and do remote calls, file I/O and message publishing outside it, using compensation rather than one big atomic block. Tradeoff: splitting the transaction means partial failure is possible and must be designed for. It bites most visibly when a slow third-party call is inside @Transactional and every concurrent request queues behind it.", level: "senior", d: ["Spring_Boot_Transactions", "Spring_Boot_AntiPatterns"] },
    { q: "How do you make a scheduled job safe to run in a horizontally scaled application?", a: "Ensure exactly-once-effect semantics: use a distributed lock such as ShedLock against a shared store, or move the job to a single-worker deployment or a leader-elected consumer, and make the job itself idempotent. Also give the scheduler a pool so one slow job does not delay others, and monitor last-success time. Tradeoff: a lock needs a shared, highly available store and does not protect against a crash after the work but before the record. It bites when the job is not idempotent, so even a correctly working lock occasionally duplicates work.", level: "senior", d: ["Spring_Boot_Async_Scheduling"] },
    { q: "What is the difference between servlet Filters and HandlerInterceptors, and when do you use each?", a: "Filters are part of the servlet specification and run around the whole DispatcherServlet, so they can wrap the request and response, touch security context, CORS or request logging, and apply to static resources. Interceptors are Spring MVC's hook, running after handler mapping and able to read handler metadata and post-handle information such as the model and view. Use filters for cross-cutting transport concerns and interceptors for MVC-specific ones. It bites when you put a filter in the wrong place, or omit it for a URL that the DispatcherServlet never serves.", level: "mid", d: ["Spring_Boot_Request_Path"] },
    { q: "How do you choose between pessimistic and optimistic locking in JPA?", a: "Optimistic locking with @Version checks the version on update and throws OptimisticLockException, so it suits read-mostly workloads with rare contention. Pessimistic locking with SELECT ... FOR UPDATE or a lock timeout suits hot rows where you need the read and the write to be indivisible, at the cost of holding locks for the transaction's duration. Tradeoff: optimistic gives better concurrency and worse retry behaviour; pessimistic gives fewer conflicts and more blocking. It bites when an application retries a conflict without re-reading, or when a lost update is possible because the entity was detached and re-saved without a version check.", level: "senior", d: ["Spring_Boot_Transactions", "Spring_Boot_Data_Model"] },
    { q: "What is a virtual thread, and how does it change Spring Boot capacity planning?", a: "Virtual threads are JVM-managed, lightweight threads that unmount from the carrier platform thread when they block, so millions can exist and blocking calls are cheap again. In Spring Boot, spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true (Java 21) makes the embedded server and executor use them, and you then stop sizing request threads and start sizing the scarce resources - the database pool, downstream rate limits, CPU. Tradeoff: you lose per-thread thread-local guarantees in some places and must propagate context explicitly. It bites when a virtual-thread service sits in front of a database pool of ten, where throughput is unchanged but memory grows.", level: "senior", d: ["Spring_Boot_Async_Scheduling", "Spring_Boot_Deployment"] },
    { q: "What happens during a graceful shutdown of a Spring Boot application?", a: "With server.shutdown=graceful, the connector stops accepting new requests, the web server drains in-flight exchanges up to spring.lifecycle.timeout-per-shutdown-phase, then beans are destroyed in reverse dependency order via @PreDestroy, DisposableBean and custom destroy methods, and the data sources and executors close. Tradeoff: the drain period lengthens pod termination, and a long-running request can exceed the timeout and be cut off. It bites when shutdown hooks do real work - draining queues, calling remote services - with no timeout budget, and the pod is killed mid-hook.", level: "mid", d: ["Spring_Boot_Deployment", "Spring_Boot_Bean_Lifecycle"] },
    { q: "How do you make an external HTTP call safely from a Spring Boot service?", a: "Wrap the client with explicit connect and read timeouts, a bounded retry policy with backoff and jitter, a circuit breaker and a concurrency bulkhead, and log or metric the outcome. Use a shared, properly configured RestClient or WebClient rather than constructing a new client per request, so pools and timeouts are uniform. Tradeoff: retries can amplify load on a struggling dependency, so they must be budgeted. It bites hardest when there is no timeout at all: one slow partner pins every request thread and the service falls over without the partner being visibly unhealthy.", level: "senior", d: ["Spring_Boot_AntiPatterns", "Spring_Boot_Async_Scheduling"] },
    { q: "What is idempotency, and how do you implement it in a Spring Boot service?", a: "An idempotent operation produces the same result whether it is applied once or many times, which is what makes retries and redeliveries safe. Implement it by accepting a client-supplied idempotency key, storing it with the result under a unique constraint, and returning the stored result on a repeat; for consumers, dedupe on the message id or business key in the same transaction as the effect. Tradeoff: it requires storage and a defined retention window, and it only helps if every writer goes through the same path. It bites when a key is scoped per request but the effect spans requests, so a retry creates a second order.", level: "senior", d: ["Spring_Boot_Messaging", "Spring_Boot_Data_Model"] },
    { q: "What is a BeanPostProcessor, and why does it matter that Boot registers some of them very early?", a: "A BeanPostProcessor gets every bean before and after its own initialisation callbacks, which is how @Autowired injection, annotation processing and proxying are implemented. Boot registers infrastructure post-processors such as the auto-configuration and configuration-property ones before your beans are created. Mechanism: registering them early means they see every bean, including the infrastructure beans, so ordering matters - PriorityOrdered post-processors run in a second, earlier pass. It bites when a custom post-processor silently does not apply to the beans you expected.", level: "mid", d: ["Spring_Boot_Bean_Lifecycle", "Spring_Boot_Startup"] },
    { q: "How do you validate configuration at startup, and what do you gain?", a: "Put jakarta.validation constraints on a @ConfigurationProperties class, register it with @ConfigurationPropertiesScan or @EnableConfigurationProperties, and make sure the validation is applied so binding fails fast. You also want constructor binding with a record or a single constructor, plus @NotNull, @Min and @Pattern constraints, and an assertion message that names the property. The gain is that a missing or malformed variable stops the deployment rather than the business. It bites when the properties class is only registered by a component scan that does not pick up plain @Component classes.", level: "mid", d: ["Spring_Boot_Config_Properties"] },
    { q: "Two beans of the same type exist and an injection point cannot choose. How do you resolve it cleanly?", a: "Mark the intended default with @Primary when one implementation is the norm, and use @Qualifier on the injection point when the choice is per-consumer. If the choice is per-environment, express it with @Profile on the implementations instead. Tradeoff: qualifiers everywhere are noise, so prefer profiles or distinct types when the variants are genuinely different things. It bites when a third-party starter adds a bean of a type you also define and your application suddenly fails to start on a version bump.", level: "junior", d: ["Spring_Boot_Startup", "Spring_Boot_AOP_Proxy"] },
    { q: "What is the Condition Evaluation Report, and when is it the fastest way to solve a startup problem?", a: "Started with --debug or debug=true, the report lists every auto-configuration considered, which conditions matched or did not, and why. It is the fastest route when a bean you expect is missing, because it distinguishes 'the auto-configuration was never imported' from 'it was imported but a condition failed'. Tradeoff: it is verbose and only covers conditions, not component scanning, so a missing @Service will not appear there. It bites when you read it the wrong way round and conclude a matching auto-configuration must have created your bean.", level: "mid", d: ["Spring_Boot_AutoConfiguration", "Spring_Boot_Startup"] },
    { q: "How do you manage configuration across environments - dev, staging, production?", a: "Keep one immutable artifact, feed it configuration at runtime, and use profiles to select overlays rather than to hold secrets. Sensible defaults live in the properties class, environment-specific values come from environment variables, mounted files or a config server via spring.config.import, and secrets are injected by the platform. Tradeoff: this means more up-front discipline because nothing is baked in. It bites when someone bakes a profile-specific file into the image, which makes every environment change a rebuild and a full rollout.", level: "junior", d: ["Spring_Boot_Config_Properties", "Spring_Boot_Deployment"] },
    { q: "When do you use @ResponseStatus, and when do you prefer an exception handler?", a: "@ResponseStatus is for a simple, stable mapping between one exception type and one status code, and it keeps the mapping on the exception itself. An @ExceptionHandler in an advice class is for everything else: validation errors with a field list, domain errors with a body, and anything that needs logging or context. Tradeoff: the handler is more powerful and also more global, so it is easy to centralise too much logic. It bites when a catch-all handler hides a status code a client depends on.", level: "junior", d: ["Spring_Boot_Layers", "Spring_Boot_AntiPatterns"] },
    { q: "What does @SpringBootTest do, and when is it the right level of test?", a: "It boots the real application context - all auto-configuration, all beans, the full filter chain - optionally with a real web server when webEnvironment is RANDOM_PORT or DEFINED_PORT. Use it when the failure you care about is wiring, a property, a security rule or a real SQL statement. Tradeoff: it is the slowest and the least diagnosable, so keep the number small and combine it with Testcontainers for anything the embedded database cannot represent. It bites when it becomes the only kind of test, because a suite that takes ten minutes stops being run.", level: "mid", d: ["Spring_Boot_Testing_Slices"] },
    { q: "What does spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto do in development and in production, and why?", a: "It controls schema management by Hibernate: create, create-drop, update, validate or none. In development create-drop is convenient; in production you want validate or none, with Flyway or Liquibase owning the schema, because update performs unversioned, unpredictable DDL and nobody can review or roll it back. Tradeoff: validate fails startup on drift, which is exactly the point. It bites when update has quietly altered a production table and the change is not in any migration file.", level: "mid", d: ["Spring_Boot_Migration_3", "Spring_Boot_Deployment"] },
    { q: "How do you trace a slow request from end to end?", a: "Start with a trace: read the latency of the endpoint from Micrometer, find the slowest span in the tracing backend, and drill into the child spans for the database, cache and remote calls. Then correlate with structured logs carrying the same trace id, and confirm the hypothesis with a query plan or a profiler. Tradeoff: this is a lot of tooling, which is why spending effort on correlation up front pays off during an incident. It bites when metrics tell you the endpoint is slow but nothing tells you which of its fifty internal steps is responsible.", level: "senior", d: ["Spring_Boot_Observability"] },
    { q: "What is the difference between @RequestBody and @RequestParam, and when does each apply?", a: "@RequestParam binds a single value from the query string, form field or path-adjacent parameter, with a required flag and a default; @RequestBody deserialises the whole request body once through a message converter, which is where JSON DTOs, validation and content negotiation apply. Use parameters for filters, pagination and simple flags, and a body for anything with structure. Tradeoff: mixing them in one handler is legal and confusing. It bites when a large query object is spread across twenty parameters and nothing validates them together.", level: "junior", d: ["Spring_Boot_Layers", "Spring_Boot_Request_Path"] },
    { q: "How do you paginate a large table efficiently?", a: "Use keyset (seek) pagination for deep pages - order by a unique, indexed column and continue from the last key seen - because OFFSET makes the database generate and discard every skipped row. Reserve offset paging for shallow pages where the UI needs absolute page numbers, and make the sort column part of an index. Tradeoff: keyset pagination cannot jump to an arbitrary page, so the user interface has to be infinite scroll or next/previous. It bites with fetch joins, where in-memory pagination silently returns a page whose size is the number of joined rows.", level: "mid", d: ["Spring_Boot_Data_Model", "Spring_Boot_Nplus1"] },
    { q: "How do you read rows into DTOs efficiently, and when is it better than loading entities?", a: "Use a JPQL constructor expression or an interface-based projection, selecting only the columns the screen needs, so no entity is managed, no lazy loading is possible and no dirty checking happens. For aggregation, a single grouped query beats loading rows into memory. Tradeoff: projections are verbose, and if the same shape is needed in twenty places a mapper over fetched entities may be simpler. It bites when a projection is introduced to fix one slow screen and then hand-written SQL spreads through the repository layer.", level: "mid", d: ["Spring_Boot_Nplus1", "Spring_Boot_Data_Model"] },
    { q: "What is a retry storm, and how do you prevent one?", a: "A retry storm is a feedback loop: a dependency slows down, clients time out and retry, the added load slows it further, and the failure amplifies instead of recovering. Prevent it with exponential backoff and jitter, a strict retry budget or a circuit breaker that stops sending requests entirely, and a bulkhead so one dependency cannot consume the whole thread pool. Tradeoff: retries improve resilience for short blips and are actively harmful during a sustained outage. It bites when a timeout is added to a client that already retries three times, giving nine attempts per request with no backoff.", level: "senior", d: ["Spring_Boot_AntiPatterns", "Spring_Boot_Deployment"] },
    { q: "How do you rate limit an endpoint, and what do you have to decide first?", a: "Decide the key (user, API key or IP), the algorithm (token bucket or leaky bucket) and where the state lives - in-memory per instance, or shared in Redis for a fleet. Return 429 with a Retry-After header, and reject early in the filter chain so you do not consume a request thread per rejected call. Tradeoff: local counters are cheap and approximate across replicas; shared counters are accurate and add a network hop. It bites with in-memory state behind a load balancer, where the effective limit is the per-instance limit multiplied by the replica count.", level: "mid", d: ["Spring_Boot_Security_JWT", "Spring_Boot_Request_Path"] },
    { q: "What is a circuit breaker, and when does it beat retries?", a: "A circuit breaker trips after a threshold of failures and fails fast without calling the dependency, then probes periodically to see whether it has recovered. It is the right tool for a sustained outage, where retries only add load. Combine it with a bulkhead so the failing dependency cannot exhaust your pool, and keep short retries for the blip case. Tradeoff: fast failure means fast errors for clients, so it needs a fallback or a degraded response, otherwise you have replaced a slow outage with a loud one.", level: "senior", d: ["Spring_Boot_AntiPatterns", "Spring_Boot_Deployment"] },
    { q: "How do you design a service to degrade gracefully instead of failing completely?", a: "Classify each dependency by whether the feature can survive without it, then supply a fallback: a cached value, a default, a reduced response or an explicit error. Return partial results rather than throwing when the API allows it, and make readiness reflect only what is truly required to serve traffic. Tradeoff: fallbacks hide degradation from monitoring unless you metric them separately, and stale data has business consequences. It bites when a fallback returns empty data that clients interpret as 'no orders' rather than 'unknown'.", level: "senior", d: ["Spring_Boot_Caching", "Spring_Boot_Observability"] },
    { q: "What is the difference between authentication and authorization, and where does each belong in Spring Security?", a: "Authentication establishes who the caller is and produces an Authentication in the SecurityContext; authorization decides what that identity may do, expressed as request matchers for URLs and @PreAuthorize for use cases. Both are enforced in the filter chain, with method security covering calls that have no HTTP entry point. Tradeoff: URL rules are coarse and easy to get wrong as the API grows, so prefer method security for the rules that matter. It bites when a new endpoint is added and is covered by a broad permitAll matcher.", level: "junior", d: ["Spring_Boot_Security_JWT"] },
    { q: "How do you secure the actuator endpoints in a container platform?", a: "Bind the management server to a separate port, or keep the base path but restrict every management matcher to authenticated roles or to the pod network, and expose only the endpoints the platform needs (health, prometheus, info). Never expose env, configprops, heapdump or shutdown publicly. Tradeoff: a separate port means a second surface to secure and to open in network policies. It bites when the readiness probe and the metrics scraper end up on the public application port because the default path was never changed.", level: "mid", d: ["Spring_Boot_Observability", "Spring_Boot_Security_JWT"] },
    { q: "Which transaction manager should you use, and when does the choice matter?", a: "JpaTransactionManager for a pure JPA/Hibernate application, DataSourceTransactionManager for plain JDBC, and a JTA manager when several resource managers must join one transaction. The distinction matters for NESTED propagation and for whether JDBC-level operations and JPA share one unit of work, and it explains what happens when you mix JDBC writes into a JPA transaction. Tradeoff: the JPA manager is convenient and hides the boundary; knowing where it ends makes surprises explainable. It bites with read-modify-write logic that mixes a native query with a JPA flush.", level: "senior", d: ["Spring_Boot_Transactions", "Spring_Boot_Data_Model"] },
    { q: "What does a read-only transaction do, and is it worth using?", a: "A transaction marked readOnly tells the persistence provider it may skip dirty checking, avoid flushing and, depending on the database, route the connection to a read replica. Spring Data also applies the hint to derived queries. Tradeoff: it is an optimisation that is easy to misuse - writing through a read-only transaction fails, and routing to a replica introduces replication lag that will surface as 'the data I just wrote is missing'. It bites when someone adds readOnly = true to a method that is only conceptually a read.", level: "mid", d: ["Spring_Boot_Transactions"] },
    { q: "How do you evolve a public API without breaking clients?", a: "Treat the contract as versioned and additive: new optional fields, tolerant readers that ignore unknown fields, and deprecation with a measured removal date rather than an immediate change. Keep the DTO separate from the entity so the schema can move underneath, and use content negotiation or a version segment only when you truly need a parallel version. Tradeoff: backward compatibility is a constraint, not a virtue - a rename that improves the code can break a client that cannot be changed. It bites when a database column rename is coupled to a JSON field rename in the same release.", level: "mid", d: ["Spring_Boot_Layers", "Spring_Boot_Migration_3"] },
    { q: "What are the options for packaging a Spring Boot application as a container image, and what does each trade?", a: "A fat jar with a JRE base is the simplest; an exploded or layered jar on a minimal base gives better layer caching and a smaller attack surface; bootBuildImage produces an OCI image with the same layering from Maven or Gradle. In all cases run as a non-root user and pass JVM options through JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS or an entrypoint. Tradeoff: a minimal base is harder to debug because there is no shell. It bites with an image that runs as root, which quietly breaks pod security policies and widens the blast radius of a container escape.", level: "mid", d: ["Spring_Boot_Deployment"] },
  ],
  glossary: [
    { term: "Auto-configuration", def: "A configuration class shipped by a library that defines beans when its conditions match what is on your classpath and what you have already defined. It backs off when you supply the bean yourself, which is the supported way to override it without excluding anything.", d: ["Spring_Boot_AutoConfiguration"] },
    { term: "Starter", def: "A dependency descriptor with no code of its own that pulls in a curated set of libraries for one job, such as spring-boot-starter-data-jpa. Starters are how auto-configuration gets onto the classpath in the first place.", d: ["Spring_Boot_AutoConfiguration", "Spring_Boot_Roadmap"] },
    { term: "@SpringBootApplication", def: "A convenience annotation combining @SpringBootConfiguration, @EnableAutoConfiguration and @ComponentScan, placed on the outermost class. Its package is the default component-scan root, which is why packages above it are invisible to the scan.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Startup"] },
    { term: "Bean", def: "An object managed by the container: the container creates it, injects its dependencies, controls its lifecycle and applies post-processing such as proxies. A plain new object is not a bean, and therefore gets no transactions, caching or validation.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Startup", "Spring_Boot_Bean_Lifecycle"] },
    { term: "ApplicationContext", def: "The central container interface, extending BeanFactory with component scanning, events, resource loading and internationalisation. It also owns the bean lifecycle and is what most application code should not depend on directly.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Startup"] },
    { term: "BeanFactory", def: "The minimal container interface for looking up beans by name or type. ApplicationContext adds the enterprise features on top, and in practice you interact with the context, not the bare factory.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Startup", "Spring_Boot_Bean_Lifecycle"] },
    { term: "Dependency injection", def: "Supplying a collaborator from outside rather than constructing it inside, which decouples the consumer from the implementation and makes the dependency explicit. In Spring this is constructor injection in almost all cases.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Layers", "Spring_Boot_Startup"] },
    { term: "Component scanning", def: "The container's discovery of @Component, @Service, @Repository and @Controller classes in the packages under the @SpringBootApplication class. It is separate from auto-configuration: your own classes are scanned, library configurations are imported.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Startup"] },
    { term: "@Configuration", def: "A class whose @Bean methods define beans. In Boot 2.2+ it defaults to proxyBeanMethods = false, so methods call each other directly instead of through an enhanced subclass of the configuration class.", d: ["Spring_Boot_AutoConfiguration"] },
    { term: "@Bean", def: "A method on a configuration class whose return value becomes a container-managed bean, with full lifecycle and post-processing. It is the imperative counterpart of stereotypes, and the way you adapt third-party classes.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Startup", "Spring_Boot_AutoConfiguration"] },
    { term: "@AutoConfiguration", def: "The Boot 3 marker for an auto-configuration class, implying @Configuration(proxyBeanMethods = false). Candidates are listed in META-INF/spring/org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration.imports, which replaced the spring.factories entry point.", d: ["Spring_Boot_AutoConfiguration", "Spring_Boot_Migration_3"] },
    { term: "@ConditionalOnMissingBean", def: "A condition that registers the bean only if the application has not already defined that type, which is the standard back-off mechanism and the reason your own DataSource wins over the auto-configured one.", d: ["Spring_Boot_AutoConfiguration"] },
    { term: "@ConditionalOnProperty", def: "A condition that matches when a property equals an expected value; a missing property counts as no match unless matchIfMissing is set. It is how features are switched on by configuration.", d: ["Spring_Boot_AutoConfiguration"] },
    { term: "BeanPostProcessor", def: "An interface invoked around each bean's initialisation - before and after its own init callbacks - so that proxies, @Autowired injection of existing beans and annotation processing can happen. It is the mechanism behind nearly every cross-cutting feature.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Bean_Lifecycle", "Spring_Boot_AOP_Proxy"] },
    { term: "BeanFactoryPostProcessor", def: "Invoked before any bean is instantiated, allowing modification of bean definitions themselves - property placeholder resolution being the canonical example. It runs earlier than BeanPostProcessors, so it cannot touch bean instances.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Bean_Lifecycle"] },
    { term: "EnvironmentPostProcessor", def: "A Boot extension point that mutates the Environment during startup, before the context is created. It is how early configuration, such as a bootstrap phase or an additional property source, is installed.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Config_Properties", "Spring_Boot_Migration_3"] },
    { term: "@PostConstruct", def: "A jakarta.annotation callback invoked after dependency injection and before InitializingBean and any custom init method. Use it for starting resources the bean owns, not for work that needs other beans that may not be built yet.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Bean_Lifecycle"] },
    { term: "DisposableBean / @PreDestroy", def: "Shutdown callbacks invoked in the order @PreDestroy, DisposableBean.destroy, then a custom destroy-method, in reverse dependency order. Anything that holds a connection, a thread or a socket should release it here.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Bean_Lifecycle"] },
    { term: "@ConfigurationProperties", def: "An annotation that binds externalised configuration to a typed object using relaxed binding, with defaults and JSR-380 validation. It replaces scattering @Value placeholders through business classes.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Config_Properties"] },
    { term: "Relaxed binding", def: "The rule set that lets spring.datasource.url, SPRING_DATASOURCE_URL and the equivalent system property all refer to the same value, with kebab-case, camelCase and underscore forms accepted in the right places.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Config_Properties"] },
    { term: "Profile", def: "A named environment dimension that selects additional configuration documents and conditions beans, activated with spring.profiles.active. Profile-specific files overlay the non-specific ones key by key, not file by file.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Config_Properties"] },
    { term: "Config Data (spring.config.import)", def: "The Boot 2.4+ mechanism for importing additional property documents, optionally, with defined precedence. It is how external files and config servers are layered without rewriting the classic application.properties lookup.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Config_Properties"] },
    { term: "Property placeholder", def: "The ${...} syntax resolved against the Environment, with defaults written ${name:default}. It is useful for small values and awkward for anything a class should not hard-code.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Config_Properties"] },
    { term: "DispatcherServlet", def: "The Spring MVC front controller that maps a request to a handler, invokes it with argument resolvers, and renders the return value. Everything in Spring MVC happens inside it, which is why filters registered around it are the right place for transport-level concerns.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Request_Path", "Spring_Boot_Layers"] },
    { term: "@RestController", def: "@Controller plus @ResponseBody at class level, so handler return values are written to the response body as JSON rather than resolved as a view name.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Layers"] },
    { term: "@RestControllerAdvice", def: "A global exception handler applying @ExceptionHandler and @ResponseBody across all controllers, the natural home for mapping domain exceptions to status codes and a consistent problem body.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Layers", "Spring_Boot_AntiPatterns"] },
    { term: "@ExceptionHandler", def: "A method that handles a specific exception type for one controller or, inside an advice class, for all of them. It must not be private, and the exception it declares is the one that triggers it.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Layers"] },
    { term: "HandlerInterceptor", def: "Spring MVC's preHandle, postHandle and afterCompletion callbacks, running inside the DispatcherServlet around handler invocation. Unlike a filter it can see handler metadata and the model, but it cannot rewrite the request before mapping.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Request_Path"] },
    { term: "Filter", def: "A servlet-spec component in the container's chain that wraps requests and responses before and after the DispatcherServlet. It is the correct place for security context, CORS, request logging, rate limiting and MDC setup.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Request_Path", "Spring_Boot_Security_JWT"] },
    { term: "DTO", def: "A data object shaped for a use case or an API contract rather than for the database. Mapping between entity and DTO keeps the schema, the domain model and the public API independently changeable, and avoids serialising lazy associations.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Layers", "Spring_Boot_Data_Model"] },
    { term: "Bean Validation (jakarta.validation)", def: "Declarative constraints such as @NotNull, @Size and @Pattern on DTO fields, enforced at the web boundary and reported as field errors. Annotate nested objects with @Valid so constraints cascade.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Layers"] },
    { term: "Connection pool", def: "A bounded set of database connections reused across requests, managed by HikariCP by default in Spring Boot. It decouples concurrency from connection creation cost, which is why its size is a capacity decision about the database rather than about the application.", d: ["Spring_Boot_AutoConfiguration", "Spring_Boot_Transactions"] },
    { term: "@TransactionalEventListener", def: "Handles an application event after the surrounding transaction commits, so a listener cannot observe - or act on - data that is about to be rolled back. It is the in-process alternative to the transactional outbox when the consumer is in the same application.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Messaging", "Spring_Boot_Transactions"] },
    { term: "ApplicationEventPublisher", def: "Publishes application events that @EventListener and @TransactionalEventListener methods receive. The mechanism is synchronous by default and runs inside the publishing thread, unless you wrap it in @Async - which is a common source of 'my listener sometimes does not run' confusion during shutdown.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Startup", "Spring_Boot_Messaging"] },
    { term: "Micrometer Observation", def: "The vendor-neutral instrumenting API in Spring Boot that produces metrics and spans from one set of instrumentation across HTTP, JDBC, caching and messaging. It is what makes tracing and metrics consistent without instrumenting every call site by hand.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Observability"] },
    { term: "Connection pool leak detection", def: "A HikariCP setting (leakDetectionThreshold) that logs the stack trace of any connection held longer than the threshold. It is the fastest way to find code that acquires a connection outside a transaction or never returns it, which otherwise shows up only as pool exhaustion.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Transactions", "Spring_Boot_Deployment"] },
    { term: "Optimistic locking (@Version)", def: "A column holding a version number that Hibernate checks on every update, throwing an OptimisticLockException if the row changed underneath. It gives good concurrency with no held locks, at the cost of failed writes that must be retried with a fresh read.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Transactions", "Spring_Boot_Data_Model"] },
    { term: "Pessimistic locking", def: "Locking selected rows for the duration of the transaction with SELECT ... FOR UPDATE, so a read-modify-write sequence is indivisible. It suits hot rows at the cost of holding locks and increasing contention and deadlock risk.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Transactions"] },
    { term: "ddl-auto", def: "The Hibernate schema-management setting (create, create-drop, update, validate, none). Development may use create-drop, but production should use validate with Flyway or Liquibase owning the schema, because update performs unreviewed and unversioned DDL.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Migration_3", "Spring_Boot_Deployment"] },
    { term: "Condition Evaluation Report", def: "The startup report produced with --debug that lists every auto-configuration considered and the outcome of its conditions. It is the fastest way to find out whether a bean was never imported or was imported and then skipped by a condition.", d: ["Spring_Boot_AutoConfiguration", "Spring_Boot_Startup"] },
    { term: "Backpressure", def: "The mechanism by which a reactive consumer tells a producer to slow down, defined in the Reactive Streams contract. Without it, a fast database source with a slow consumer buffers without limit - which is how an unbounded queue turns a traffic spike into an out-of-memory failure.", d: ["Spring_Boot_WebFlux"] },
    { term: "Rolling update", def: "A deployment that replaces pods gradually while old and new versions serve traffic simultaneously. It requires backward compatible schema changes, graceful shutdown, and readiness gating, because for part of the rollout two different versions of the code are live.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Deployment", "Spring_Boot_Migration_3"] },
    { term: "Sidecar or init container", def: "Kubernetes patterns for supporting processes: an init container runs migrations or waits for a dependency before the app starts, and a sidecar runs alongside it. They exist because coordination inside a single JVM is impossible once the work is cross-cutting.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Deployment"] },
    { term: "Cucumber-style end-to-end test", def: "A test that exercises a running system the way a client does, over HTTP and through the real security chain. It is the highest-fidelity and slowest test, and it belongs at the boundaries where the wiring between components can only be verified by running them.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Testing_Slices", "Spring_Boot_Security_JWT"] },
    { term: "Micrometer context propagation", def: "A library that copies registered thread-local values (MDC, the SecurityContext, tracing context) across thread and Reactor boundaries, so an async or reactive hop keeps the same trace and user. It complements a TaskDecorator, which is the hook for imperative executors.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Async_Scheduling", "Spring_Boot_Observability"] },
    { term: "@Valid vs @Validated", def: "@Valid is the specification annotation used to trigger cascading validation of a request object; @Validated is Spring's annotation that enables method-level constraint validation on a class and supports validation groups. Using the wrong one in a service usually means the constraint is never evaluated.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Layers"] },
    { term: "Spring Data repository", def: "An interface extending CrudRepository or JpaRepository that the framework implements with a runtime proxy, deriving queries from method names, annotations or @Query. There is no generated class and no implementation to write.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Layers", "Spring_Boot_Data_Model"] },
    { term: "EntityManager / Hibernate Session", def: "The JPA unit-of-work object that holds the persistence context, manages the first-level cache, flushes changes and participates in the transaction. In a web request its lifetime is the transaction, unless Open Session in View extends it.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Transactions", "Spring_Boot_Nplus1"] },
    { term: "First-level cache (persistence context)", def: "The per-transaction identity map of managed entities, which guarantees identity within a transaction and drives dirty checking. It explains why loading the same entity twice gives one instance and one query.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Nplus1", "Spring_Boot_Transactions"] },
    { term: "Second-level cache", def: "A shared or cross-JVM entity cache configured with a region factory and @Cacheable on entities. It is off by default, invalidation is coarse, and it is rarely worth the operational cost in a service with several replicas.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Caching"] },
    { term: "N+1 select", def: "One query to load a list plus one query per row to initialise a lazy association. It is the most common performance defect in JPA applications and shows up as log volume that grows with the result size.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Nplus1"] },
    { term: "Join fetch / @EntityGraph", def: "Two declarative ways to fetch a related association in the same SQL statement instead of a second one. Both may produce duplicate rows for to-many associations and both need care with paging.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Nplus1"] },
    { term: "LAZY vs EAGER", def: "Whether an association is loaded with the owning query or through a proxy on first access. JPA defaults @ManyToOne to EAGER and @OneToMany to LAZY, an asymmetry that quietly shapes your query load.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Data_Model", "Spring_Boot_Nplus1"] },
    { term: "Open Session in View", def: "The Boot default that keeps the EntityManager bound to the request thread so lazy loading still works during response rendering. It hides LazyInitializationException at the cost of holding a pooled connection for the whole request.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Nplus1", "Spring_Boot_Transactions"] },
    { term: "Batch fetching", def: "Telling Hibernate to load collections or entities in batches with IN clauses via hibernate.default_batch_fetch_size or @BatchSize, which collapses an N+1 into a small fixed number of statements at the cost of memory.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Nplus1"] },
    { term: "Transaction (ACID)", def: "A unit of work whose changes commit atomically and either all become visible or none do, with isolation defining what concurrent transactions can see. In Spring it is managed by a PlatformTransactionManager bound to the thread.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Transactions"] },
    { term: "Propagation REQUIRED", def: "The default propagation: join the current transaction if one exists, otherwise create it. Commit and rollback happen at the outermost boundary, which is why an inner failure poisons the whole unit of work.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Transactions"] },
    { term: "Propagation REQUIRES_NEW", def: "Suspends the current transaction and starts an independent one on a second connection, committing even if the outer transaction later rolls back. Use it for records that must survive failure, and budget for the extra connection.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Transactions"] },
    { term: "Propagation NESTED", def: "Uses a JDBC savepoint inside the current transaction so the inner part can roll back independently without a second connection. It requires savepoint support from the driver and transaction manager, and it is not a real independent transaction.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Transactions"] },
    { term: "Rollback-only", def: "A flag marking a participating transaction as doomed, set when an inner participant fails. Catching the exception in application code does not clear it, and the eventual commit fails with UnexpectedRollbackException.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Transactions"] },
    { term: "Self-invocation", def: "Calling a method of the same object from inside that object, which bypasses any Spring proxy. It silently disables @Transactional, @Async, @Cacheable and @Retryable on the inner method, which is why those annotations must live on externally invoked public methods.", d: ["Spring_Boot_AOP_Proxy", "Spring_Boot_Transactions"] },
    { term: "JDK dynamic proxy vs CGLIB", def: "Two ways to build the AOP proxy: JDK proxies implement interfaces and cannot reach class members, CGLIB subclasses the target and can override non-final methods. Spring Boot defaults to CGLIB (proxyTargetClass = true), so final classes and methods silently lose their advice.", d: ["Spring_Boot_AOP_Proxy"] },
    { term: "Pointcut", def: "The expression selecting which join points advice applies to. Spring's AspectJ-style pointcuts match execution of methods, and the match is resolved at proxy creation time, which is why the target must be known when the proxy is built.", d: ["Spring_Boot_AOP_Proxy"] },
    { term: "Cache abstraction", def: "Spring's Cache and CacheManager interfaces that let @Cacheable work with Caffeine, Redis or any other backend. Separating the annotation from the implementation means a cache can be swapped without touching business code.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Caching"] },
    { term: "Caffeine", def: "The modern in-memory cache library Boot uses for a simple local cache, with size and expiry bounds that a naive ConcurrentMapCacheManager lacks. Because it is per-JVM, entries and evictions are not shared between replicas.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Caching"] },
    { term: "@Cacheable / @CacheEvict", def: "Annotations implemented as cache advice on a proxy: @Cacheable reads through the cache, @CachePut overwrites and @CacheEvict removes entries, all requiring that the method is invoked through the proxy. Values are stored only on successful return.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Caching"] },
    { term: "Cache-aside", def: "The default caching pattern: read from the cache, and on a miss query the database and populate the cache. The application owns invalidation, which is both the flexibility and the failure mode, since a missed eviction serves stale data until the TTL expires.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Caching"] },
    { term: "Flyway / Liquibase", def: "Schema migration tools that apply versioned SQL or changesets and record what has run. They should be a deployment step rather than application startup, so rolling deploys do not race for DDL locks.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Deployment", "Spring_Boot_Migration_3"] },
    { term: "HikariCP", def: "The connection pool Boot auto-configures, fast and well instrumented with Micrometer metrics and a leak detector. maximumPoolSize is a decision about total database capacity, not a performance knob to raise under pressure.", d: ["Spring_Boot_AutoConfiguration", "Spring_Boot_Deployment"] },
    { term: "Actuator", def: "The starter that adds production endpoints - health, info, metrics, env, loggers, prometheus, and more. Only health and info are exposed over HTTP by default; anything else must be enabled explicitly, ideally on a separate management port.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Observability"] },
    { term: "Micrometer", def: "The metrics facade behind Spring Boot's instrumentation, recording counters, timers, gauges and distributions into a MeterRegistry. It is vendor-neutral, and cardinality is the discipline it forces on you.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Observability"] },
    { term: "Prometheus / OpenMetrics", def: "The scrape-based metrics format and ecosystem most Spring Boot deployments use; adding micrometer-registry-prometheus exposes /actuator/prometheus for the scraper. Pull-based collection means the application does not need to know where the backend is.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Observability"] },
    { term: "OpenTelemetry", def: "A vendor-neutral standard for traces, metrics and logs, used in Spring Boot through Micrometer Tracing and an OTel SDK with an OTLP exporter. It brings context propagation so spans from different services join into one trace.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Observability"] },
    { term: "Span / trace", def: "A span is one timed operation within a trace; the trace is the whole request across services. Putting the trace and span identifiers in log lines is what connects a stack of messages to the actual call tree.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Observability"] },
    { term: "Graceful shutdown", def: "Stop accepting new requests, finish in-flight ones within a timeout, then destroy beans and close resources, configured with server.shutdown=graceful and spring.lifecycle.timeout-per-shutdown-phase. In Kubernetes it pairs with a preStop delay and a termination grace period.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Deployment", "Spring_Boot_Bean_Lifecycle"] },
    { term: "Liveness and readiness probes", def: "Liveness decides whether to restart a process and must only check things a restart fixes; readiness decides whether to send traffic and may check dependencies. Mixing them turns a database blip into a fleet-wide restart.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Observability", "Spring_Boot_Deployment"] },
    { term: "Virtual thread", def: "A lightweight JVM-managed thread (Java 21) that unmounts from its carrier when it blocks, enabled in Boot with spring.threads.virtual.enabled. Capacity planning shifts from thread counts to the genuinely scarce resources such as the database pool.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Async_Scheduling"] },
    { term: "TaskDecorator", def: "A hook that wraps task execution so you can capture and restore thread-local state - MDC, the SecurityContext, request attributes - and clear it afterwards. Without it, async and scheduled work runs with empty or stale context.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Async_Scheduling"] },
    { term: "@Async / TaskExecutor", def: "@Async is proxy advice that submits a call to an executor and returns immediately; the executor is what actually bounds concurrency. The default is uncapped, which is why production defines a ThreadPoolTaskExecutor with a bounded queue and a rejection policy.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Async_Scheduling"] },
    { term: "@Scheduled / TaskScheduler", def: "Annotations for cron and fixed-delay execution, driven by a TaskScheduler that is single-threaded by default, so one slow job delays all others. In a scaled deployment the job also needs a distributed lock or a single-worker design.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Async_Scheduling"] },
    { term: "Transactional outbox", def: "Writing a domain change and an intent-to-publish row in one database transaction, with a separate relay publishing to the broker. It removes the dual-write gap, at the cost of at-least-once delivery, a small delay and more moving parts.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Messaging"] },
    { term: "At-least-once delivery", def: "The realistic broker guarantee: a message may be delivered more than once after a failure, restart or rebalance. It is harmless only when consumers are idempotent, so deduplication belongs in the consumer's transaction.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Messaging"] },
    { term: "Idempotency", def: "The property that applying an operation repeatedly has the same effect as applying it once, usually implemented with a client-supplied key and a unique constraint. It is what makes retries, redeliveries and network timeouts safe to handle automatically.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Messaging", "Spring_Boot_Data_Model"] },
    { term: "Spring Security filter chain", def: "The ordered list of security filters wrapped by a FilterChainProxy, covering CSRF, authentication, authorization and exception translation. Because the whole model is filters and matchers, a rule on a path that is never requested is a rule that never runs.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Security_JWT"] },
    { term: "SecurityContext / SecurityContextHolder", def: "The holder for the current Authentication, kept per thread for the duration of a request and cleared afterwards. In a stateless API the context is rebuilt from the bearer token on every request, which is why it must never leak across pooled threads.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Security_JWT"] },
    { term: "CSRF", def: "Cross-site request forgery protection, enabled by default because browsers attach cookies automatically. It is unnecessary for a purely stateless bearer-token API and essential for anything that authenticates with a cookie.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Security_JWT"] },
    { term: "JWT", def: "A signed, self-contained token whose claims are verified by signature and expiry rather than by a session lookup. It removes per-request session state but requires careful key rotation, clock-skew handling and revocation strategy.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Security_JWT"] },
    { term: "OAuth2 resource server", def: "A service that validates tokens issued by someone else, configured in Boot with a jwk-set-uri or a public key plus issuer validation. It brings the standard filter chain and key refresh, and leaves claim-to-role mapping to you.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Security_JWT"] },
    { term: "Method security", def: "Declarative authorization inside the service layer with @PreAuthorize, @PostAuthorize and @EnableMethodSecurity, protecting use cases that have no HTTP entry point. It is proxy-based too, so self-invocation bypasses it just like @Transactional.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Security_JWT", "Spring_Boot_AOP_Proxy"] },
    { term: "Reactive Streams / Reactor", def: "The standard (Publisher, Subscriber, backpressure) and its Reactor implementation (Mono, Flux, operators) that WebFlux is built on. Backpressure is part of the contract, which is why collecting a Flux eagerly defeats the point.", d: ["Spring_Boot_WebFlux"] },
    { term: "Blocking the event loop", def: "Calling a blocking API from a non-reactive thread, which stalls every other request sharing that loop. The symptom is high latency at low CPU, and the fixes are R2DBC or an explicit hop to boundedElastic with a bounded pool.", d: ["Spring_Boot_WebFlux"] },
    { term: "WebFlux", def: "Spring's reactive web stack, built on Reactor Netty and non-blocking I/O. It is the right choice when concurrency per instance matters and every dependency can be non-blocking, and the wrong one when most work is CPU-bound or JDBC-based.", d: ["Spring_Boot_WebFlux"] },
    { term: "Jakarta EE", def: "The Java EE standard renamed to Jakarta, with the namespace moving from javax to jakarta in version 10 and the minimum Java version rising to 11. Spring Boot 3 targets Jakarta EE 9+ on Java 17, which is why every javax import must be migrated.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Migration_3"] },
    { term: "Expand-migrate-contract", def: "The safe schema evolution pattern: add compatible structures, migrate data in batches, then remove what is no longer needed once no old version runs. It is the foundation of a rolling deployment that can actually be rolled back.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Migration_3", "Spring_Boot_Deployment"] },
    { term: "Hibernate 6", def: "The JPA provider shipped with Spring Boot 3, with rewritten internals, new type handling and its own naming and schema tooling. It is stricter about what it accepts, so entity mappings and dialect-specific behaviour that worked under Hibernate 5 need re-checking after an upgrade.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Migration_3", "Spring_Boot_Data_Model"] },
    { term: "JVM container awareness", def: "The JVM's ability to size the heap from a cgroup memory limit, configured with -XX:MaxRAMPercentage. Native memory such as metaspace, direct buffers and thread stacks sits outside the heap, so a limit must leave headroom or the container is killed with exit code 137 and no Java error at all.", d: ["Spring_Boot_Deployment", "Spring_Boot_Async_Scheduling"] }
  ]
};
